window.CONTENT={"version":"0.9.1","verses":{"an-nisa-4-63":{"ar":"اُولٰۤىِٕكَ الَّذِيْنَ يَعْلَمُ اللّٰهُ مَا فِيْ قُلُوْبِهِمْ فَاَعْرِضْ عَنْهُمْ وَعِظْهُمْ وَقُلْ لَّهُمْ فِيْٓ اَنْفُسِهِمْ قَوْلًا ۢ بَلِيْغًا","id":"Mereka itulah orang-orang yang Allah ketahui apa yang ada di dalam hatinya. Oleh karena itu, berpalinglah dari mereka, nasihatilah mereka, dan katakanlah kepada mereka perkataan yang membekas pada jiwanya.","en":"Those are the people whose hearts Allah knows. So turn away from them, advise them, and speak to them words that leave a deep mark on their souls.","src":"QS. An-Nisa [4]:63 · Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},"al-ahzab-33-70":{"ar":"يٰٓاَيُّهَا الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوا اتَّقُوا اللّٰهَ وَقُوْلُوْا قَوْلًا سَدِيْدًاۙ","id":"Wahai orang-orang yang beriman, bertakwalah kamu kepada Allah dan ucapkanlah perkataan yang benar.","en":"O you who believe, be mindful of Allah and speak words that are true.","src":"QS. Al-Aḥzāb [33]:70 · Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},"an-nisa-4-5":{"ar":"وَلَا تُؤْتُوا السُّفَهَاۤءَ اَمْوَالَكُمُ الَّتِيْ جَعَلَ اللّٰهُ لَكُمْ قِيٰمًا وَّارْزُقُوْهُمْ فِيْهَا وَاكْسُوْهُمْ وَقُوْلُوْا لَهُمْ قَوْلًا مَّعْرُوْفًا","id":"Janganlah kamu serahkan kepada orang-orang yang belum sempurna akalnya harta (mereka yang ada dalam kekuasaan)-mu yang Allah jadikan sebagai pokok kehidupanmu. Berilah mereka belanja dan pakaian dari (hasil harta) itu dan ucapkanlah kepada mereka perkataan yang baik.","en":"Do not hand over to those whose reasoning is not yet mature the property (of those under your authority) that Allah has made the basis of your livelihood. Provide for them from it, clothe them, and speak to them words that are good.","src":"QS. An-Nisa [4]:5 · Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},"taha-20-44":{"ar":"فَقُوْلَا لَهٗ قَوْلًا لَّيِّنًا لَّعَلَّهٗ يَتَذَكَّرُ اَوْ يَخْشٰى","id":"Berbicaralah kamu berdua kepadanya (Fir‘aun) dengan perkataan yang lemah lembut, mudah-mudahan dia sadar atau takut.","en":"Speak to him (Pharaoh), the two of you, with gentle words, so that perhaps he will become aware or afraid.","src":"QS. Ṭāhā [20]:44 · Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},"al-isra-17-28":{"ar":"وَاِمَّا تُعْرِضَنَّ عَنْهُمُ ابْتِغَاۤءَ رَحْمَةٍ مِّنْ رَّبِّكَ تَرْجُوْهَا فَقُلْ لَّهُمْ قَوْلًا مَّيْسُوْرًا","id":"Jika (tidak mampu membantu sehingga) engkau (terpaksa) berpaling dari mereka untuk memperoleh rahmat dari Tuhanmu yang engkau harapkan, ucapkanlah kepada mereka perkataan yang lemah lembut.","en":"If (you are unable to help, and so) you (are compelled to) turn away from them while seeking the mercy from your Lord that you hope for, then say to them words that are gentle.","src":"QS. Al-Isrā’ [17]:28 · Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},"al-isra-17-23":{"ar":"۞ وَقَضٰى رَبُّكَ اَلَّا تَعْبُدُوْٓا اِلَّآ اِيَّاهُ وَبِالْوَالِدَيْنِ اِحْسٰنًاۗ اِمَّا يَبْلُغَنَّ عِنْدَكَ الْكِبَرَ اَحَدُهُمَآ اَوْ كِلٰهُمَا فَلَا تَقُلْ لَّهُمَآ اُفٍّ وَّلَا تَنْهَرْهُمَا وَقُلْ لَّهُمَا قَوْلًا كَرِيْمًا","id":"Tuhanmu telah memerintahkan agar kamu jangan menyembah selain Dia dan hendaklah berbuat baik kepada ibu bapak. Jika salah seorang di antara keduanya atau kedua-duanya sampai berusia lanjut dalam pemeliharaanmu, sekali-kali janganlah engkau mengatakan kepada keduanya perkataan “ah” dan janganlah engkau membentak keduanya, serta ucapkanlah kepada keduanya perkataan yang baik.","en":"Your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him, and that you be good to your parents. If one or both of them reach old age in your care, never say to them a word of “ugh” (impatience), do not snap at them, and speak to them words that are good.","src":"QS. Al-Isrā’ [17]:23 · Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"}},"framework":{"title":"The Module’s Integrative Framework","columns":["Unit","Pragmatics focus","Qaulan principle (proposed pairing)","Panca Cinta pillar"],"rows":[{"unit":2,"focus":"Deixis, Reference & Inference","qaulan":[{"name":"Baligha","ref":"An-Nisa 4:63","gloss":"clear, effective speech that reaches the listener","verse":"an-nisa-4-63"}],"pillars":["Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia"],"note":""},{"unit":3,"focus":"Entailment & Presupposition","qaulan":[{"name":"Sadida","ref":"Al-Ahzab 33:70","gloss":"truthful, correct speech","verse":"al-ahzab-33-70"}],"pillars":["Cinta Ilmu"],"note":""},{"unit":4,"focus":"Cooperative Principle & Implicature","qaulan":[{"name":"Baligha","ref":"deepened from Unit 2","gloss":"effective, well-aimed speech","verse":"an-nisa-4-63"}],"pillars":["Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia"],"note":""},{"unit":5,"focus":"Speech Acts","qaulan":[{"name":"Ma’rufa","ref":"An-Nisa 4:5","gloss":"good, fitting speech","verse":"an-nisa-4-5"}],"pillars":["Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya"],"note":""},{"unit":6,"focus":"Politeness & Impoliteness Cross-Culturally","qaulan":[{"name":"Layyina","ref":"Taha 20:44","gloss":"gentle speech","verse":"taha-20-44"},{"name":"Maysura","ref":"Al-Isra 17:28","gloss":"easy speech","verse":"al-isra-17-28"}],"pillars":["Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia"],"note":"Ma’rufa in support"},{"unit":7,"focus":"Discourse & Pragmatic Research","qaulan":[{"name":"Sadida","ref":"","gloss":"accuracy in reporting others’ words"}],"pillars":["Cinta Ilmu"],"note":"Baligha in support; Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia in support"},{"unit":8,"focus":"Pragmatics in Language Teaching","qaulan":[{"name":"Karima","ref":"Al-Isra 17:23","gloss":"good speech, in a mentoring role","verse":"al-isra-17-23"}],"pillars":["Cinta Ilmu"],"note":"Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia in support"},{"unit":9,"focus":"Capstone: Cross-Cultural Digital Communication","qaulan":[{"name":"All six","ref":"","gloss":"synthesis of all six qaulan principles"}],"pillars":["All five pillars"],"note":"as an integrated whole; Cinta Lingkungan and Cinta Tanah Air enter through the capstone scenarios"}],"pillarList":["Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya","Cinta Ilmu","Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia","Cinta Lingkungan","Cinta Tanah Air"]},"info":{"about":{"title":"About the App","blocks":[{"type":"image","src":"assets/logo.svg","alt":"The app icon: an eight-pointed star holding a chat bubble with typing dots","caption":"The app icon: an eight-pointed star (a traditional geometric motif) holding a chat bubble with typing dots, for adab in digital talk."},{"type":"p","text":"This app is the companion to the module **Pragmatics for Adab-Based Digital Communication: A Module Integrating Kurikulum Cinta for Indonesian Islamic Higher Education Students**. It puts the nine units on screen, so that you can read, write your answers, and keep your own record in one place."},{"type":"h2","num":"","title":"What it does"},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**Nine units.** Each unit pairs a pragmatic lens with a qaulan principle and a Panca Cinta pillar, and moves from a Digital Snapshot of a short chat to a Field Journal in which you record a real interaction. Unit 9 is the capstone project.","**Your own work.** Type your answers into the boxes, tables and checklists. They are saved automatically and can be exported as a PDF.","**Writing help.** Optional sentence starters for your answers, pop-up definitions for key terms, short “Did you know?” tips, and a searchable glossary.","**One Field Journal for the semester.** Entries from every unit are kept together, and can be exported as CSV, Word or PDF.","**Framework map.** Table 1 as an interactive map of qaulan principles and Panca Cinta pillars across the units."]},{"type":"h2","num":"","title":"Good to know"},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**It works offline.** The app does not need the internet and does not send your work anywhere. Your answers stay on this computer.","**Qur’anic text.** Each verse is shown in Arabic with its Indonesian translation, and the English translation is based on Tafsir Kemenag (quran.kemenag.go.id).","**Other people’s privacy.** Retype and anonymize any real message before you save it. Use roles or initials instead of names, and leave out phone numbers and screenshots."]},{"type":"h2","num":"","title":"The five Panca Cinta pillars"},{"type":"pillars"},{"type":"note","text":"Offline desktop app for Windows."}]},"help":{"title":"How to Use the App","topics":[{"id":"start","title":"Quick start","keywords":"begin first steps open unit","blocks":[{"type":"list","style":"number","items":["Open **Home** at the top, then choose **Start here**. It shows the nine units and how the app works. Or choose a unit number.","Read the **Overview**: the three lenses and the learning outcomes.","Move through the stages. Use the tabs under the menu, **Next stage** on the Home tab, or the **Previous** and **Next** buttons at the bottom of each page.","Write your answers in the boxes. They save automatically.","Come back later with **Continue** on the Start here page, which takes you to where you stopped.","When you finish, select **Export my work (PDF)** at the top right."]}]},{"id":"move","title":"Finding your way around","keywords":"menu top bar tabs stages references navigate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The **menu at the top** has five tabs: Home, Write, Export, View and Help. It stays folded to save room: select a tab to open it, and it folds away after you choose something. The unit you are in is shown in the middle of the bar. On the **Home** tab, numbers 1 to 9 open the units, each on its Overview."},{"type":"p","text":"The **tabs along the top** are the stages of the open unit, followed by **References** at the end. Select a tab to move to it. A unit may include extra tabs, such as a practice stage or a project tracker."},{"type":"p","text":"At the top of the menu are two tools that work across units: the **Framework map (Table 1)** and **My Field Journal**. Below the units are **About the App**, **How to Use the App** and **Developers**."},{"type":"p","text":"Each unit card on **Start here** shows how many of its stages you have opened, and a tick when you have opened them all. The unit numbers on the **Home** tab show the same: a ring for a unit you have started and a filled number with a tick for a finished one. Stage tabs you have opened carry a small tick."}]},{"id":"look","title":"The menu, themes and the ribbon","keywords":"ribbon menu tabs theme dark light pin keep open view appearance","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The menu works like a ribbon in Word, folded by default so that the page has as much room as possible."},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**Open a tab.** Select Home, Write, Export, View or Help. Press **Esc**, or select the tab again, to fold it away.","**Keep it open.** Double-click a tab, or tick **Keep the ribbon open** on the View tab. Double-click again to fold it.","**Keyboard.** Use the left and right arrow keys to move between tabs and the down arrow to enter the ribbon.","**Light or dark.** On the **View** tab choose the app theme (Follow Windows, Light or Dark) and, separately, the ribbon style (Match the app, Light or Dark).","**Export my work (PDF)** stays at the top right at all times. PDFs are always printed on white, whichever theme you use.","**Text size.** On the **View** tab choose Normal, Large or Larger. It changes the text of the unit pages."]}]},{"id":"write","title":"Writing and saving your answers","keywords":"save autosave boxes type lost data computer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Every box, table cell and tick box saves as you type. You will see **Saved** with the time when you select **Your name** at the top right. You do not need a save button, and closing the app is safe."},{"type":"p","text":"Your work is stored in this app, on this computer, for your Windows user account. It does not travel to another computer. If you change computers, export a PDF first."},{"type":"note","text":"In the browser version, your work is stored in this browser on this device. Use the same browser to come back to it, and export a PDF to keep a copy."},{"type":"note","text":"Tip: use **Your name** at the top right if you want your name printed on the PDF. It is optional."}]},{"id":"snippets","title":"Writing help: starters, terms and tips","keywords":"starters sentence terms definitions pop-up glossary tips did you know hints","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four small helpers sit inside the units. All of them are optional."},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**Sentence starters.** Click inside any answer box. A small **Starters** button appears above its right corner. Select it, choose an opening such as “The clue that points to this meaning is…”, and carry on typing. The starter is only a prompt: change it or delete it.","**Key terms.** Terms with a dotted underline, such as *implicature*, open a short definition when you select them. Each term is underlined once on a page. Select **Open the unit** in the pop-up to go to the unit that introduces it.","**Did you know?** Short tips and mini-examples appear in some stages. They are extra reading and do not need an answer.","**Glossary.** **Glossary**, on the Home and Write tabs, lists every key term, with a search box."]},{"type":"p","text":"To switch starters or underlined terms off, open the **Write** tab and untick them. Starters you insert are part of your answer and appear in your PDF; tips and pop-ups do not."}]},{"id":"pdf","title":"Exporting your work as a PDF","keywords":"pdf export print save file name","blocks":[{"type":"list","style":"number","items":["Open the unit you want to export.","Optional: select **Your name** at the top right and type it there.","Select **Export my work (PDF)**.","Choose where to save the file, and give it a name."]},{"type":"p","text":"The PDF contains your answers for that unit, the journal entries you wrote for it, your Practice result (Unit 4) and your project tracker (Unit 9). When you are in **My Field Journal**, the same button exports the journal instead."}]},{"id":"journal","title":"Using My Field Journal","keywords":"journal entry entries csv word docx anonymize edit delete search filter","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Field Journal is one journal for the whole semester. You can add an entry from the last stage of a unit, or from **My Field Journal** with **New entry**."},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**Date** is filled in for you. **Time** is optional: write it in 24-hour format, for example 14:30.","**Unit** tags the entry. Choose *General* if it does not belong to one unit.","**Platform** is where the exchange happened, for example WhatsApp.","The four boxes follow the log in your module. Their labels change slightly from unit to unit.","Under **See an example entry** in the form you will find a filled-in sample for the unit. It is only a guide: write your own, anonymized."]},{"type":"p","text":"Before an entry is saved, the app checks for names after titles, phone numbers, handles, emails and web links, and warns you if it finds one. The check is a helper only: you decide, and it cannot catch everything, so read your entry again."},{"type":"p","text":"In **My Field Journal** you can edit or delete an entry (the app asks before it deletes), filter by unit, and search the text. **Export CSV** opens in Excel, **Export Word** creates a .docx file, and **Export PDF** creates a PDF. Each export contains the entries shown."}]},{"id":"map","title":"Using the Framework map (Table 1)","keywords":"framework table 1 qaulan pillar panca cinta verse","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Table 1 shows how each unit pairs a pragmatics focus with a qaulan principle and a Panca Cinta pillar. Select a **qaulan** or a **pillar** to light up the units where it appears. Units where it appears *in support* are shaded differently."},{"type":"p","text":"When you select a qaulan, the verse that anchors it appears below the table. Select a **unit number** in the table to open that unit."}]},{"id":"extras","title":"Practice quiz and project tracker","keywords":"quiz literal implied tracker project capstone progress","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"**Unit 4, Practice:** judge the last message of each exchange as literal or implied, and, for implied messages, choose the most likely meaning. The explanation appears after each answer. Select **Try this one again** or **Start again** to repeat. The score is for you only."},{"type":"p","text":"**Unit 9, Tracker:** set a status, a target date and notes for each of the eight workflow steps, and tick the five requirements as they are ready. A step that is past its target date and not done is marked."}]},{"id":"verses","title":"Qur’anic anchors","keywords":"verse arabic kemenag translation tafsir","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each anchor shows the verse in Arabic, the Indonesian translation (Terjemah Kemenag), and an English translation based on Tafsir Kemenag. The source line ends every anchor: **Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id**."}]},{"id":"trouble","title":"If something looks wrong","keywords":"problem help error missing dark mode word export","blocks":[{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**My answers are not there.** Check that you are signed in to the same Windows account and that the Saved note appeared under **Your name** at the top right.","**The text is small or too large.** Use your Windows display scaling settings.","**The colours changed.** The app follows the light or dark setting of Windows.","**A save window did not appear.** Look for it behind the app, then try again."]}]}]},"developers":{"title":"Developers","intro":"This app was developed by:","people":[{"name":"Coky Fauzi Alfi","role":"Developer"},{"name":"Susi Herti Afriani","role":"Developer"}],"credits":["Qur’anic text and Indonesian translation: Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id. English translation based on Tafsir Kemenag.","Fonts: Amiri, Noto Sans and Noto Serif, used under the SIL Open Font License."]}},"glossary":[{"term":"pragmatics","match":["pragmatics"],"unit":1,"def":"The study of meaning in context: what speakers mean by what they say, and how hearers work it out."},{"term":"adab","match":["adab"],"unit":1,"def":"Good conduct and courtesy in how we treat others. In this module it is the ethical lens for how we write and reply online."},{"term":"Kurikulum Cinta","match":["Kurikulum Cinta","Kurikulum Berbasis Cinta"],"unit":1,"def":"Kurikulum Berbasis Cinta, the framework of the Ministry of Religious Affairs (Kemenag) built around the five Panca Cinta pillars (Keputusan Direktur Jenderal Pendidikan Islam Nomor 6077 Tahun 2025)."},{"term":"Panca Cinta","match":["Panca Cinta"],"unit":1,"def":"The five pillars of the Kurikulum Cinta: Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya, Cinta Ilmu, Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia, Cinta Lingkungan, and Cinta Tanah Air."},{"term":"qaulan","match":["qaulan"],"unit":1,"def":"Qaul is Arabic for speech or saying. The module works with six Qur’anic qualities of speech (baligha, sadida, ma’rufa, layyina, maysura, karima) and pairs each with a pragmatic idea in Table 1.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},{"term":"register","match":["register","registers"],"unit":1,"def":"The way language changes with situation, audience and purpose, for example a formal message to a lecturer versus a chat with a friend."},{"term":"hedge","match":["hedge","hedges","hedged","hedging","softener","softeners"],"unit":1,"def":"A word or phrase that softens a statement or request, such as “maybe”, “I think” or “I was wondering if…”."},{"term":"deixis","match":["deixis","deictic"],"unit":2,"def":"Expressions whose referent shifts with the context of utterance, such as I, you, here, now and tomorrow (Levinson, 1983; Yule, 1996)."},{"term":"deictic center","match":["deictic center","deictic centre"],"unit":2,"def":"The speaker’s “I, here, now”. When a reader’s time or place differs from the writer’s, the center shifts."},{"term":"entailment","match":["entailment","entailments","entails","entail"],"unit":3,"def":"A relation in which the truth of one sentence guarantees the truth of another. “Rani finished her assignment” entails “Rani did some work.”"},{"term":"presupposition","match":["presupposition","presuppositions","presupposes","presuppose"],"unit":3,"def":"A background assumption a sentence takes for granted. It typically persists when the sentence is negated or turned into a question."},{"term":"Cooperative Principle","match":["Cooperative Principle"],"unit":4,"def":"People generally communicate expecting others to contribute meaningfully to the exchange (Grice, 1975)."},{"term":"maxim","match":["maxim","maxims"],"unit":4,"def":"One of Grice’s four conversational expectations: Quantity (be as informative as needed), Quality (say what you believe is true), Relation (be relevant), Manner (be clear) (Grice, 1975)."},{"term":"implicature","match":["implicature","implicatures"],"unit":4,"def":"Meaning that is suggested or inferred rather than directly stated."},{"term":"flouting a maxim","match":["flouting a maxim","flouts a maxim","flouted","flouting","flout","flouts"],"unit":4,"def":"A speaker deliberately appears to break a conversational expectation so that the hearer looks for an additional meaning."},{"term":"speech act","match":["speech act","speech acts"],"unit":5,"def":"An action performed by saying something, such as requesting, promising, apologizing or refusing."},{"term":"locution","match":["locution"],"unit":5,"def":"What is literally said. The first of three levels of an utterance (Austin, 1962; Yule, 1996)."},{"term":"illocution","match":["illocutionary force","illocutionary","illocution"],"unit":5,"def":"What the speaker does by saying it, for example requesting or refusing. It depends on convention and context (Austin, 1962; Yule, 1996)."},{"term":"perlocution","match":["perlocutionary","perlocution"],"unit":5,"def":"The effect an utterance has on the hearer. It depends on the hearer (Austin, 1962; Yule, 1996)."},{"term":"felicity conditions","match":["felicity conditions","felicity condition"],"unit":5,"def":"The conditions under which an act succeeds: propositional content, preparatory, sincerity and essential (Searle, 1969)."},{"term":"indirect speech act","match":["indirect speech act","indirect speech acts","indirect act","indirect acts"],"unit":5,"def":"One act performed by means of another, for example a question in form that works as a request in function (Searle, 1975)."},{"term":"force-indicating devices","match":["force-indicating devices","force-indicating device"],"unit":5,"def":"Signals of which act is being performed, such as word order, performative verbs, intonation and, in writing, punctuation and emoji."},{"term":"face","match":["positive face","negative face"],"unit":6,"def":"Public self-image. Positive face is the want to be approved of; negative face is the want to be unimpeded (Brown & Levinson, 1987)."},{"term":"face-threatening act","match":["face-threatening act","face-threatening acts","FTA","FTAs"],"unit":6,"def":"An act, such as a request, refusal or criticism, that puts someone’s face at risk. The risk grows with social distance, power difference and degree of imposition (Brown & Levinson, 1987)."},{"term":"politeness","match":["politeness"],"unit":6,"def":"As a strategy, politeness redresses a threat to face (Brown & Levinson, 1987). As etiquette (tatakrama), it is what a community agrees is proper (Mislikhah, 2014)."},{"term":"impoliteness","match":["impoliteness"],"unit":6,"def":"Behavior that attacks face and is intended or perceived as such (Culpeper, 1996, 2011)."},{"term":"address term","match":["address term","address terms","terms of address"],"unit":6,"def":"A word used to speak to or about someone directly: a name, a title or a pronoun such as Bapak, Ibu, Anda or kamu."},{"term":"speech situation","match":["speech situation"],"unit":7,"def":"The setting of an utterance, made up of seven elements: speaker and addressee, context, purpose, speech act, utterance, time and place (Wiryotinoyo, 2006)."},{"term":"pragmatic unit","match":["pragmatic unit"],"unit":7,"def":"An illocution combined with a proposition (Wiryotinoyo, 2006)."},{"term":"heuristic analysis","match":["heuristic analysis"],"unit":7,"def":"Hypothesize the force of an utterance, test it against the Gricean maxims and the context, and revise it until an implicature is identified. Results stay probabilistic (Wiryotinoyo, 2006)."},{"term":"pragmalinguistic failure","match":["pragmalinguistic failure"],"unit":8,"def":"Choosing the wrong linguistic form for an intended speech act, for example “Give me the file” instead of “Could I have the file, please?” (Thomas, 1983)."},{"term":"sociopragmatic failure","match":["sociopragmatic failure"],"unit":8,"def":"Misjudging the social distance or power that should govern the choice of form, for example treating a professor as casually as a classmate (Thomas, 1983)."},{"term":"instructed pragmatics","match":["instructed pragmatics"],"unit":8,"def":"Research on whether pragmatic competence can be taught and which methods work best (Taguchi, 2015)."},{"term":"noticing hypothesis","match":["noticing hypothesis"],"unit":8,"def":"Learners are more likely to acquire a pattern once they consciously notice it and can talk about it."},{"term":"Qaulan Baligha","match":["qaulan baligha","qaulan balīgha"],"unit":2,"def":"Clear, effective speech that reaches the listener. One of the six qaulan principles in Table 1; it is paired with Unit 2.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},{"term":"Qaulan Sadida","match":["qaulan sadida"],"unit":3,"def":"Truthful, correct speech. One of the six qaulan principles in Table 1; it is paired with Unit 3.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},{"term":"Qaulan Ma’rufa","match":["qaulan ma’rufa","qaulan ma'rufa"],"unit":5,"def":"Good, fitting speech. One of the six qaulan principles in Table 1; it is paired with Unit 5.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},{"term":"Qaulan Layyina","match":["qaulan layyina"],"unit":6,"def":"Gentle speech. One of the six qaulan principles in Table 1; it is paired with Unit 6.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},{"term":"Qaulan Maysura","match":["qaulan maysura"],"unit":6,"def":"Easy speech. One of the six qaulan principles in Table 1; it is paired with Unit 6.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"},{"term":"Qaulan Karima","match":["qaulan karima"],"unit":8,"def":"Good speech, in a mentoring role. One of the six qaulan principles in Table 1; it is paired with Unit 8.","src":"Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"}],"starters":{"generic":[{"label":"Explain your reading","items":["In my reading, the writer means that …","This could also be read as …","The clue that points to this meaning is …","I am fairly sure because …, but it could change if …"]},{"label":"Adab check","items":["A more courteous way to say this would be …","This might hurt or embarrass the reader because …","To keep the relationship respectful, I would …"]},{"label":"Reflect","items":["I noticed that …","Before this unit I thought …; now I think …","Next time I will …"]}],"units":{"1":[{"label":"Describe the message","items":["The message was sent on … between … and …","The writer seems to want …","Without knowing the context, a reader might think …"]},{"label":"Context and tone","items":["The tone feels … because of the word(s) …","The emoji / punctuation changes the meaning by …","The relationship between the two people is probably …"]}],"2":[{"label":"Who, where, when","items":["“I / you / we” refers to …","“Here / there” refers to …","“Now / tomorrow” is counted from …","A reader who opens this later will understand … as …"]},{"label":"Repair the message","items":["To make the reference clear, I would write …","The unclear word is … because …"]}],"3":[{"label":"Entailment","items":["This sentence entails that …","If the sentence is true, then … must also be true.","It does not entail that …"]},{"label":"Presupposition","items":["This sentence takes for granted that …","The trigger word is … ","Even if the sentence is negated, it still presupposes …"]}],"4":[{"label":"Say and imply","items":["What is said: …","What is implied: …","The maxim that seems flouted is … (Quantity / Quality / Relation / Manner) because …"]},{"label":"Test the reading","items":["I think the speaker expects the hearer to infer … because …","The reply could be literal if …","A reader who does not share this context might understand …"]}],"5":[{"label":"Name the act","items":["By writing this, the speaker is …ing (requesting / refusing / apologizing / promising).","The literal act is …, but the actual act is …","The words that signal this act are …"]},{"label":"Effect and conditions","items":["The effect on the hearer is probably …","The act might not succeed because the condition … is not met.","In writing, the missing tone is replaced by …"]}],"6":[{"label":"Face and distance","items":["This act threatens the hearer’s … face because …","The social distance between them is …, and the power difference is …","The degree of imposition is high / low because …"]},{"label":"Politeness choices","items":["The strategy used here is …","In my community, this would sound … because …","A more fitting version for a lecturer would be …"]}],"7":[{"label":"Describe the situation","items":["The speaker is … and the addressee is …","The purpose of the exchange is …","The context that matters most is …"]},{"label":"Analyze","items":["My first hypothesis about the force is …","The contextual evidence for it is …","This is a hypothesis, not a proof, because …"]}],"8":[{"label":"Spot the problem","items":["This is a pragmalinguistic failure because the form … does not fit the act …","This is a sociopragmatic failure because the speaker misjudged …","The sentence is grammatical, but it sounds … to a lecturer."]},{"label":"Plan teaching","items":["I would help learners notice … by …","A short practice task would be …","I would check the practice material by …"]}],"9":[{"label":"Analyze the misunderstanding","items":["The two readings are … and …","The misunderstanding comes from …, not from a lack of respect.","The pragmatic issue is …"]},{"label":"Revise and explain","items":["I changed … to … because …","The Panca Cinta pillar this scenario engages most is …, because …","To avoid generalizing, we wrote …"]}]}},"units":[{"id":"unit01","number":1,"title":"Getting to Know Pragmatics and Our Digital World","titleId":"Mengenal Pragmatik dan Dunia Digital Kita","lead":"Every act of digital communication carries two layers of meaning: what the words literally say, and what the sender actually intends the reader to understand. Pragmatics is the study of that second layer: meaning as it is shaped by context, relationship, and shared assumptions, rather than by grammar and vocabulary alone. For students who spend a large part of their academic and social lives typing rather than speaking, this gap between said and meant is not an abstract linguistic curiosity; it is the source of most real misunderstandings with lecturers, classmates, and strangers online.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Semantic vs. pragmatic meaning; the inferential model","qaulan":"The Qur’anic typology of speech, mapped in Table 1","pancaCinta":"The five pillars of Kurikulum Cinta, mapped in Table 1"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish semantic meaning (what words denote) from pragmatic meaning (what a speaker intends and a hearer infers)."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Identify at least three features that make digital communication a distinct pragmatic environment (reduced paralinguistic cues, asynchronicity, context collapse)."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Explain, in your own words, how the module’s three lenses (pragmatic theory, the Qur’anic qaulan typology, and the Panca Cinta pillars of Kurikulum Cinta) will be used together across the module."},{"role":"Production","text":"Produce the same communicative intent across three digital registers (casual, academic-formal, public) and justify the differences."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Begin a semester-long Field Journal of naturally occurring digital exchanges."}],"stages":[{"id":"table1","tag":"Table 1","title":"The Module’s Integrative Framework","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This table is the module’s “map.” It is not fully explained here: each row is developed in its own unit. Introducing it in Unit 1 lets you see the whole terrain before you enter it, and gives your lecturer a single reference point to return to across the semester. Beside the map, one adab term recurs in every unit: *tabayyun*, verifying before acting or sharing."},{"type":"framework"},{"type":"term","label":"About this table","paras":["The pairing of pragmatic constructs with the six qaulan (Qur’anic speech) principles is an original integrative device proposed for this module. It is not part of the official Kurikulum Berbasis Cinta document, which centers on the five Panca Cinta pillars (Kementerian Agama RI, 2025, Keputusan Direktur Jenderal Pendidikan Islam Nomor 6077 Tahun 2025). Both layers are shown here so the mapping’s provenance stays clear to readers and reviewers.","That document is written for madrasah. This module adapts its five topics (Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya, Cinta Ilmu, Cinta Lingkungan, Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia, and Cinta Tanah Air) for Islamic higher education and calls them “pillars” where the document says “topics” (*topik*)."]}]},{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This unit introduces pragmatics through a single, deliberately simple digital misunderstanding, then uses that example to preview every concept the module will return to across Units 2–9, and to introduce the framework that ties pragmatic accuracy to adab (Islamic communicative ethics) throughout the module."},{"type":"p","text":"Read the exchange below, which was constructed for this module. What happened here, and why did it go wrong?"},{"type":"chat","title":"Rani (student) and Dr. Hakim (lecturer)","sub":"constructed for this module","messages":[{"who":"Rani (student)","side":"sent","text":"Assalamu’alaikum, Pak. Mohon maaf mengganggu waktunya. Untuk tugas Pragmatik minggu ini, apakah boleh saya kumpulkan besok pagi? Saya masih menyelesaikan bagian analisis.","gloss":"Peace be upon you, sir. Sorry to disturb you. For this week’s Pragmatics assignment, may I submit it tomorrow morning? I’m still finishing the analysis section.","time":"20:41"},{"who":"Dr. Hakim (lecturer)","side":"recv","text":"Wa’alaikumsalam. Bisa aja sih, tapi usahakan jangan telat-telat ya.","gloss":"Literally, “Bisa aja sih, tapi usahakan jangan telat-telat ya” means something close to “It’s possible, I suppose, but try not to be late”: a hedge, not a clear yes or no.","time":"20:52"},{"note":"Next day, 10:04 — Rani submits the assignment"},{"who":"Dr. Hakim (lecturer)","side":"recv","text":"Rani, ini kok baru masuk jam segini? Saya kira kamu cuma tanya pendapat saya, bukan minta izin resmi buat telat.","gloss":"Rani, why is this only coming in at this hour? I thought you were only asking my opinion, not formally asking permission to be late.","time":"10:11"}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 1.1","text":"The Snapshot: a constructed exchange between a student and a lecturer. Note the timestamps."},{"type":"h2","num":"","title":"Your first readings"},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Before any discussion, write down your answers to the two questions below. They will be collected without being resolved yet: this pair of readings is exactly what Stage 2 will examine."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-readings","prompts":[{"id":"rani","label":"If you were Rani, what would you have understood Dr. Hakim to mean?"},{"id":"hakim","label":"If you were Dr. Hakim, what did you intend?"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Work through the exchange with these four questions."},{"type":"list","style":"number","items":["What was literally said in Dr. Hakim’s reply?","What did Rani infer, and what evidence in the text led her there?","What did Dr. Hakim intend?","Why did the gap occur?"]},{"type":"term","label":"Key term · illocutionary force (preview)","paras":["The act a sentence performs when it is uttered. The same sentence can perform different acts: granting, withholding, or simply commenting. Hedged language leaves that act underdetermined. The term is covered formally in Unit 5."]},{"type":"p","text":"Complete this comparison table as a class. The first row is done for you."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-compare","header":["","Rani’s reading","Dr. Hakim’s intention"],"rows":[{"example":true,"cells":["What was said","“Bisa aja sih…”","“Bisa aja sih…”"]},{"cells":["What it was taken to do",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["What made that reading available",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["If Dr. Hakim’s actual intention was to withhold clear permission, what would a response closer to *qaulan sadida* (clear, correct, truthful speech) have looked like, without losing courtesy?","This is the first appearance of a claim the module will return to repeatedly: politeness and clarity are not opposites. A courteous reply can still be unambiguous, and an unclear reply is not made more respectful by being softened; it simply relocates the misunderstanding.","A second adab habit applies to Rani as well: *tabayyun*, verifying before acting or sharing. Rani could have checked with a short follow-up question, such as “Jadi boleh, Pak?” [[(So it is allowed, sir?)]], before treating a hedge as permission."]},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"A message can be grammatically perfect and still misfire. The same “Okay.” can mean agreement, annoyance, or just “received”, depending on who sends it, when, and what came before.","lines":[["Sent right after a long request","Okay."],["Possible readings","agreeing · reluctant · simply acknowledging"]]}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"These are the core ideas of the unit. The Snapshot is the running illustration throughout."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Semantic meaning vs. pragmatic meaning"},{"type":"p","text":"Semantics asks what a sentence means in isolation; pragmatics asks what a speaker means by using that sentence, here, now, with this hearer. “Bisa aja sih” has one semantic meaning and at least two live pragmatic readings."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"The inferential model of communication"},{"type":"p","text":"Communication is not simply encoding and decoding a fixed message (the “code model”). Hearers actively infer speaker meaning from what is said, what is left unsaid, and what they assume about the speaker’s intentions. Rani’s misreading was not a comprehension failure; it was a reasonable inference given the available cues."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"What makes digital communication a distinct pragmatic space"},{"type":"p","text":"Brainstorm first, then consolidate around these four features:"},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["**Reduced paralinguistic cues.** There is no tone of voice, facial expression, or pause length to disambiguate a hedge.","**Asynchronicity.** An 11-minute reply gap (as in the Snapshot) can itself carry meaning (hesitation? busy? considering the request?) that a face-to-face exchange would not.","**Context collapse.** The same message may be read by an audience the sender did not fully anticipate (a WhatsApp reply meant privately, screenshotted and shared).","**New disambiguating resources.** Emoji, punctuation repetition, read receipts, and typing indicators partly substitute for lost paralinguistic cues, and will reappear as data in later units (especially Units 4 and 5, on implicature and speech acts)."]},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"Preview of the module’s toolkit"},{"type":"p","text":"Here are the four analytical tools you will acquire, named but not yet taught. The Snapshot already contains material for all four."},{"type":"table","id":"s3-toolkit","header":["Unit 2","Unit 3","Unit 4","Unit 5"],"rows":[{"cells":["Reference and deixis","Entailment and presupposition","The Cooperative Principle and implicature","Speech act theory"]}],"strip":true}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Here is the same communicative intent, asking for a short deadline extension, expressed across three digital registers. What changes, and why?"},{"type":"table","id":"s4-registers","header":["Register","Message"],"rows":[{"cells":["To a close friend (chat)","“bro tugas pragmatik blm kelar, deadline kelar besok kan? mepet bgt wkwk”\n[[(bro, I haven’t finished the Pragmatics assignment; the deadline is still tomorrow, right? it’s really tight, lol)]]"]},{"cells":["To a lecturer (formal message)","“Assalamu’alaikum, Pak/Bu. Mohon izin bertanya, apakah tenggat pengumpulan tugas Pragmatik dapat diperpanjang hingga besok pagi? Saya masih menyelesaikan bagian analisis data. Terima kasih atas pengertiannya.”\n[[(Peace be upon you, Sir/Madam. May I ask whether the deadline for the Pragmatics assignment can be extended until tomorrow morning? I am still finishing the data analysis section. Thank you for your understanding.)]]"]},{"cells":["In a public class group (semi-formal, public audience)","“Assalamu’alaikum, teman-teman. Ada yang tahu apakah Bapak/Ibu dosen memberi kelonggaran waktu untuk tugas minggu ini? Terima kasih infonya”\n[[(Peace be upon you, everyone. Does anyone know whether the lecturers are allowing some leeway on time for this week’s assignment? Thanks for the info.)]]"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"task","label":"Discuss","paras":["• Which linguistic features signal register: greeting formulas, sentence length, use of “mohon izin,” emoji density, directness of the request?","• Why does the public group message not ask the lecturer directly, even though the goal is the same?","• Which version most clearly embodies *qaulan sadida*, and which most clearly embodies *qaulan layyina* (gentle speech)? Is there a version that manages to do both?"]},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Typing dots, read receipts and emoji are digital versions of tone of voice and eye contact. They add meaning that the words alone do not carry."}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Choose one real communicative intent from your own academic life this week (for example, asking for feedback, declining a group project role, or apologizing for a late reply) and write it in the same three registers as Stage 4. Write in the language you would really use for it (Indonesian, English, or mixed), with an English gloss if it is not English."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-produce","header":["Register","Your version","English gloss (if needed)"],"rows":[{"cells":["Chat to a friend",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Message to a lecturer",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Public class group",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"note","text":"**SELF/PEER REVIEW RUBRIC**"},{"type":"table","id":"s5-rubric","header":["Criterion","Look for"],"rows":[{"cells":["Pragmatic appropriateness","Does the register match the relationship and platform?"]},{"cells":["Clarity (qaulan sadida)","Would the reader know exactly what is being asked or stated?"]},{"cells":["Courtesy (qaulan layyina / ma’rufa)","Is the tone fitting to the relationship, without sacrificing clarity?"]},{"cells":["Register control","Are the three versions genuinely distinct, not just longer or shorter?"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"adab","label":"Peer step","paras":["Exchange drafts with a partner and identify one place where clarity and courtesy pull in different directions. Then revise together."]}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Field Journal is a semester-long task. Each week, log at least one real digital exchange (your own, or one you witnessed, anonymized: no names, phone numbers, or identifying details) where you noticed a gap between what was said and what was meant. **This week’s entry:** log a moment resembling the Rani–Dr. Hakim exchange from your own life."},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"How the moment resembles the Rani–Dr. Hakim exchange","example":{"time":"14:30","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"Chat with Lecturer A about my draft. I wrote first; this is the reply, retyped.","said":"“Silakan dikirim dulu, nanti saya lihat kalau sempat.” (Please send it first; I’ll look at it if I have time.)","meant":"A friendly reply, but I think it meant the draft is not a priority this week. It did not say no, and it did not give a day.","focus":"Like the Rani–Dr. Hakim exchange, the polite surface hid a different message: “kalau sempat” (if I have time) left the deadline open, so I could not tell what to do next."}},{"type":"note","text":"**Exchange:** platform, your relationship to the other person by role (such as friend or lecturer), and the retyped, anonymized text. **What was said:** the words as they appeared. **What I think was meant.** **Unit focus:** the answer to that week’s prompt; this week, how the moment resembles the Rani–Dr. Hakim exchange. Units change only the Unit focus prompt, never the columns."},{"type":"term","label":"Before you log · protecting other people","paras":["• Anonymize all identifying details. Do not submit screenshots containing another person’s name, number, or photo: only retyped, anonymized text.","• Use only exchanges you took part in or could legitimately see, never private chats of third parties or closed groups you do not belong to.","• Replace names, handles, numbers, photos, institutions, and places with role labels (Friend A, Lecturer). A lecturer or a small class is easy to identify, so anonymize by role, not by initial.","• Entries stay with you and the instructor. Entries pooled for the Unit 7 Snapshot, or shown to the class, need your agreement, with the other party fully anonymized.","• Class work is not research: any publication from student data needs institutional ethics clearance first.","• These entries become the raw material for the mini discourse analysis task in Unit 7."]},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 2 returns to the Snapshot’s digital surface features (timestamps, “segini” (this), “kamu” (you), “besok” (tomorrow)) as data for deixis and reference: how digital text points to people, places, and times without the shared physical context that anchors face-to-face deixis."}]}],"references":[]},{"id":"unit02","number":2,"title":"Pointing Without Pointing","titleId":"Deiksis, Referensi, dan Inferensi dalam Wacana Digital","subtitle":"Deixis, Reference, and Inference in Digital Discourse","lead":"In Unit 1 you saw that what a message says and what its sender means can differ. This unit looks at one small, everyday source of that gap: words such as *here*, *tomorrow*, and *you*, whose meaning depends on who typed them, when they were typed, and where they are read.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Deixis, reference, and inference","qaulan":"Qaulan baligha: clear, effective speech. QS. An-Nisa 4:63","pancaCinta":"Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Tell deixis, reference, and inference apart, and separate pointing that reaches out to the situation (exophoric) from pointing back to earlier text (anaphoric)."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Explain how timestamps, platforms, group chats, and messages that have scrolled out of view move the deictic center."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Connect clear reference to qaulan baligha and to Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia: choosing words so that the other person does not have to guess, and asking before guessing."},{"role":"Production","text":"Find each deictic expression in a short exchange, say what it refers to and which clue shows this, and rewrite the message so its key references can be recovered, once informally and once formally."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Log one exchange in which a pointing word could be read in two ways."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dr. Hakim posts in the class WhatsApp group at 23:52, the day after Rani’s late submission in Unit 1. Rani reads the post the next morning."},{"type":"chat","title":"Pragmatics Class — group chat","sub":"constructed for this module","messages":[{"who":"Dr. Hakim","side":"recv","text":"“Kalian yang kemarin mengirimnya ke sana, tolong kirim ulang ke sini besok. Ini yang terakhir.”","gloss":"Those of you who sent it there yesterday, please resend it here tomorrow. This is the last time.","time":"23:52"},{"who":"Rani","side":"sent","text":"“Baik, Pak. Saya kirim hari ini.”","gloss":"Understood, Sir. I will send it today.","time":"06:40"}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 2.1","text":"The Rani–Dr. Hakim thread continues. Note both timestamps."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Before judging anyone, list every word in Dr. Hakim’s message and in Rani’s reply that cannot be understood without knowing *who* sent it, *when*, and *where*. Keep your list; you will use it in Stage 2."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-list","prompts":[{"id":"list","label":"Your list"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complete the table for the words on your list. For each expression, write the readings it could have and the clue in the context that lets a reader choose between them. The first row is done for you."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-deixis","header":["Expression","Possible referents","Clue in the context"],"rows":[{"example":true,"cells":["“besok” (tomorrow)","The calendar day after 23:52, or the day after the reader sees the post","The timestamp, and when the reader opens the chat"]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"Then compare Dr. Hakim’s “besok” [[(tomorrow)]] with Rani’s “hari ini” [[(today)]]. Could they name the same day? What would Rani have needed to know to be sure?"},{"type":"responses","id":"s2-compare","prompts":[{"id":"compare","label":"Your answer"}]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["A reader who acts on a guessed reading without checking, for example by resubmitting to the wrong place, lapses in *tabayyun* (verifying before acting or sharing). But a sender who writes a message that only works if the reader shares the sender’s clock and screen has also fallen short of *qaulan baligha*. Responsibility is shared."]},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"In a group chat, “you” can mean one person or everyone. When it is unclear, readers often assume someone else will answer."}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This stage gives you the vocabulary for what you just did by instinct. The Snapshot message is the running example throughout."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Deixis and its five types"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · deixis","paras":["Expressions whose referent shifts with the context of utterance. The *deictic center* is the speaker’s “I, here, now”; when a reader’s time or place differs from the writer’s, the center shifts (Levinson, 1983; Yule, 1996; Fillmore, 1997)."]},{"type":"table","id":"s3-types","header":["Type","Examples","The digital twist"],"rows":[{"cells":["Person","I, you, we","In Indonesian, kita includes the addressee and kami excludes them. In a group chat that decides whether a reader is included in an obligation or merely informed of it."]},{"cells":["Time","now, today, tomorrow","A timestamp turns “tomorrow” into a calculation: a message sent at 23:52 and read at 06:40 can place “tomorrow” on two different calendar days."]},{"cells":["Place","here, there, this","“Send it here, not there” is meaningless without knowing which platforms the sender has in mind."]},{"cells":["Discourse","this chapter, the above","“The message above” points into a thread that may have scrolled away."]},{"cells":["Social","Bapak/Ibu versus kamu","Forms that encode relative status. The choice tells the reader how the sender sees the relationship."]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"Reference is an act; inference is the reader’s share"},{"type":"p","text":"Reference is not a property of a word. It is something a speaker *does* with a word. A reader succeeds only by inferring which entity the speaker had in mind, from the word, the surrounding text, and shared knowledge (Yule, 1996; Brown & Yule, 1983). The same expression can therefore succeed for one reader and fail for another."},{"type":"p","text":"Computational linguistics calls this the coreference problem. In the well-known Winograd schema, “The trophy does not fit in the suitcase because it is too big,” a reader knows that *it* is the trophy only through world knowledge, not grammar (Levesque, Davis, & Morgenstern, 2012). You exercise the same skill whenever you write “make it shorter” to an AI chatbot and need the chatbot to know what “it” is."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"Pointing outward or pointing back"},{"type":"p","text":"An expression can point outward to the situation (*exophoric*: “Look at this!” with an attached image) or backward to earlier text (*anaphoric*: “that” = the message above) (Halliday & Hasan, 1976). Where “that” points to a whole message rather than to an entity, it is often called discourse deixis, one of the five types above. In both cases, ask where the answer sits: in the world, in the text, or nowhere the reader can reach."},{"type":"p","text":"In a long thread, quote-replies, scrolling, and notifications cut the link between “that” and its antecedent. A reply such as “I agree with that,” read in a notification preview, has lost the very message it points to."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"When reference breaks down: guess or ask"},{"type":"p","text":"When the intended referent cannot be recovered, the reader either guesses or asks. A clarification question is the pragmatic *repair* that restores shared reference. On platforms where the audience is mixed or imagined (Marwick & boyd, 2011), “you” and “those people” may be read by people the writer never had in mind. Vague posts such as “Sebagian dari kalian perlu belajar sopan santun” [[(Some of you need to learn manners)]] or “Mereka tahu siapa mereka” [[(They know who they are)]] leave every reader to infer whether they are the target."},{"type":"p","text":"A polite repair, such as “Maaf Pak, yang dimaksud ‘di sini’ itu grup ini atau LMS?” [[(Sorry Sir, by ‘here’ do you mean this group or the LMS?)]], is the deixis-level form of *tabayyun*: it verifies before acting."},{"type":"define","label":"Try it on any message · the three-question check","paras":["**Who** is it?   ·   **Where** is it?   ·   **Counted from when?**"]},{"type":"anchor","verse":"an-nisa-4-63","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan baligha","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: a deictic expression whose referent only the sender can recover fails the listener even when the sender is sincere. Choosing words so the other person does not have to guess, and asking before guessing, is a concrete form of care (Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia)."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Because a deictic expression is only as clear as the shared context behind it, and digital platforms constantly separate sender from reader in time and place, practicing qaulan baligha by anchoring “you,” “here,” and “tomorrow” explicitly, and by asking before assuming when someone else’s anchor is unclear, becomes an expression of Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia, sparing the reader the cost of guessing."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Indonesian marks a difference that English “we” does not. *Kita* includes the listener; *kami* excludes the listener. Choosing the wrong one in a group chat can leave someone feeling left out.","lines":[["Kita harus kumpul besok.","We (including you) must meet tomorrow."],["Kami akan kumpul besok.","We (not including you) will meet tomorrow."]]}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Your intention stays the same: *to make sure a classmate resubmits the assignment to the right place by the right time.* Write it three times. The versions differ in how much they rely on deixis."},{"type":"table","id":"s4-registers","header":["Register","How much deixis?","Your version"],"rows":[{"cells":["Chat to a friend","Heavy. It relies on shared context.\nFor example: “Kirim ke sana aja nanti malam” [[(send it there tonight)]]",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Public class-group post","Some, but the addressee and the platform are named.",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Formal email to the lecturer","Deixis replaced by explicit names, dates, and platforms.\nFor example: “paling lambat Senin, 12 Oktober, pukul 23.59 WIB, melalui LMS mata kuliah” [[(by Monday, 12 October, 23:59 WIB, through the course LMS)]]",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"task","label":"Reflect","paras":["How much deixis can each register afford, and why? Which version would you trust most if you read it at 06:40 on your phone?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-reflect","prompts":[{"id":"reflect","label":"Your reflection"}]}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Take a short exchange from your own group chat or social media feed that contains at least three deictic expressions. Use only exchanges you took part in or could legitimately see. **Retype it and replace names, handles, and places with role labels** (Friend A, Lecturer); never submit a screenshot. Then annotate each deictic expression and rewrite the message so that its key references can be recovered without guessing."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-annotate","header":["Expression","Type","Referent","Clue that identifies it","Inference required"],"rows":[{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"tall":true},{"type":"responses","id":"s5-rewrite","prompts":[{"id":"rewrite","label":"Your rewrite, with its key references recoverable"}]},{"type":"adab","label":"Before you hand in · the rubric","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s5-rubric","items":["Each deictic expression is classified by the correct type.","The exophoric/anaphoric distinction is used correctly.","Each referent is supported by evidence from the context.","The rewrite embodies qaulan baligha without removing every deictic expression and becoming stiff."]}}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Log one digital exchange in which a pointing word could be read two ways. In the *Unit focus* column, name the pointing word, write down the two readings, the clue you used to choose, and the clue that was missing. This builds on the “what was said / what I think was meant” habit from Unit 1 by narrowing it to one kind of unstated meaning: *who, where, and when*."},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"The pointing word, its two readings, the clue you used, and the clue that was missing","example":{"time":"09:15","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"Class group chat. Classmate A wrote to the whole group, retyped.","said":"“Kita ketemu di sini jam 3 ya.” (Let’s meet here at 3, okay?)","meant":"“di sini” (here) could be the library entrance or the canteen; “jam 3” was most likely 15:00.","focus":"Pointing word: “di sini” (here). Reading 1: the library entrance. Reading 2: the canteen where we met last week. Clue I used: an earlier message about returning books. Missing clue: no place name or map pin."}},{"type":"note","text":"**Exchange:** platform, your relationship to the other person by role (friend, lecturer, classmate), and the retyped, anonymized text. **Unit focus this week:** the pointing word, its two readings, the clue you used, and the clue that was missing."},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 3 asks what a sentence takes for granted before it makes its claim. It reuses the definite reference you met here (“the policy,” “his decision”)."}]}],"references":["Birner, B. J. (2013). *Introduction to pragmatics*. Wiley-Blackwell.","Brown, G., & Yule, G. (1983). *Discourse analysis*. Cambridge University Press.","Fillmore, C. J. (1997). *Lectures on deixis*. CSLI Publications.","Halliday, M. A. K., & Hasan, R. (1976). *Cohesion in English*. Longman.","Hanks, W. F. (2005). Explorations in the deictic field. *Current Anthropology, 46*(2), 191–220.","Huang, Y. (2014). *Pragmatics* (2nd ed.). Oxford University Press.","Levesque, H. J., Davis, E., & Morgenstern, L. (2012). The Winograd schema challenge. In *Proceedings of the Thirteenth International Conference on Principles of Knowledge Representation and Reasoning (KR 2012)*. AAAI Press.","Levinson, S. C. (1983). *Pragmatics*. Cambridge University Press.","Marwick, A. E., & boyd, d. (2011). I tweet honestly, I tweet passionately: Twitter users, context collapse, and the imagined audience. *New Media & Society, 13*(1), 114–133.","Yule, G. (1996). *Pragmatics*. Oxford University Press."]},{"id":"unit03","number":3,"title":"Reading What’s Implied","titleId":"Membaca yang Tersirat: Entailment dan Presupposition","subtitle":"Entailment and Presupposition","lead":"In Unit 2 you learned to identify what an expression refers to. This unit asks a different question: what does a sentence take for granted before it asserts its actual claim? The object of analysis shifts from reference to underlying assumption.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Entailment and presupposition","qaulan":"Qaulan sadida: truthful, correct speech. QS. Al-Ahzab 33:70","pancaCinta":"Cinta Ilmu (love of knowledge)"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish entailment (guaranteed by a sentence, and not cancellable without contradiction) from presupposition (a background assumption that typically persists under negation and questioning), and both from implicature (cancellable and context-dependent; previewed here, covered in Unit 4)."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Explain how presupposition triggers (definite descriptions, factive verbs, change-of-state verbs, “again,” clefts) present an unstated, unverified claim as common ground in headlines, captions, and comment replies."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Connect presupposition to qaulan sadida and Cinta Ilmu: a text can mislead even when every sentence in it is technically true."},{"role":"Production","text":"Separate a text’s asserted from its presupposed content, test the presupposition under negation and questioning, and rewrite the text so the presupposed claim becomes explicit and verifiable, once in a public register and once in an analytical one."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Log one headline, caption, or comment whose more questionable claim is presupposed rather than asserted; name the trigger and the fact the text assumed rather than argued."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Rani–Dr. Hakim thread continues. On the evening of Rani’s reply in Unit 2, a classmate posts the message below."},{"type":"chat","title":"A classmate’s post","sub":"constructed for this module","messages":[{"who":"Classmate","side":"recv","text":"“Bahkan Rani pun tidak bisa menyelesaikan tugas Pak Hakim tepat waktu.”","gloss":"Even Rani could not finish Dr. Hakim’s assignment on time.","time":"That evening"}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 3.1","text":"A classmate’s post about Rani. The sentence does more than report one fact."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Before judging whether this claim is fair, state what the sentence already assumes to be true:","• about the **class**,","• about **Rani**,","• and about the **difficulty of the assignment**."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-assumes","prompts":[{"id":"assumes","label":"What the sentence assumes"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Set the **asserted content** of the classmate’s post against its **presupposed content**. Asserted content is what the sentence says outright. Presupposed content is what it takes for granted. The first row is done for you."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-ap","header":["Asserted content","Presupposed content (taken for granted)"],"rows":[{"example":true,"cells":["Rani did not finish the assignment on time.","There is an assignment, set by Dr. Hakim, with a deadline."]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true}]}]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["Reposting or reacting to this sentence without first checking whether the assignment was actually unusually difficult may itself be a small lapse in *tabayyun* (verifying before acting or sharing), even though you never personally asserted that claim.","Ask yourself: if I like, share, or answer this post, which of its assumptions am I helping to spread?"]}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This stage gives you the tools for what you just did by instinct. The classmate’s post is the running example throughout."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Semantic entailment"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · entailment","paras":["A relation in which the truth of one sentence guarantees the truth of another, regardless of speaker, context, or belief. For example, “Rani finished her assignment” entails “Rani did some work.”"]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** This is the same relation tested by AI fact-checking and chatbot source-grounding tools, which flag a claim as “supported,” “contradicted,” or “unverified” against a document. In natural language processing the task is known as recognizing textual entailment (Dagan, Glickman, & Magnini, 2006; Zeng, Abumansour, & Zubiaga, 2021). One caveat: that task uses a looser standard than the strict relation taught here, counting a claim as entailed when a typical reader would infer it from the source (Dagan et al., 2006). Entailment is therefore not merely a classroom logic exercise; it is the relation that both careful readers and machines rely on to check whether a shared post actually follows from its source."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"Presupposition and the negation and question tests"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · presupposition","paras":["A background assumption a sentence takes for granted. Unlike an assertion, it typically persists when the sentence is negated or turned into a question. For example, both “Dr. Hakim regrets his policy” and “Dr. Hakim does not regret his policy” presuppose that a policy exists."]},{"type":"table","id":"s3-tests","header":["Test","How it works"],"rows":[{"cells":["Negation test","Negate the sentence. If the background claim is still taken for granted, it is presupposed, not asserted."]},{"cells":["Question test","Turn the sentence into a question. If the background claim is still taken for granted, it is presupposed."]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Comment sections and quote-posts often exploit this property: a person who argues against “the professor’s controversial policy” still inherits and spreads the presupposition that the policy is controversial, without ever having agreed to that claim."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"Presupposition triggers"},{"type":"p","text":"Triggers are specific words or constructions known to generate presuppositions (Levinson, 1983; Yule, 1996; Huang, 2014):"},{"type":"table","id":"s3-triggers","header":["Trigger","Examples"],"rows":[{"cells":["Definite descriptions","“the policy,” “his decision”"]},{"cells":["Factive verbs","know, regret, realize"]},{"cells":["Change-of-state verbs","stop, start"]},{"cells":["Iterative expressions","again"]},{"cells":["Cleft constructions","“It was the deadline that caused the confusion.”"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"Indonesian has its own triggers: *masih* [[(still)]], *sudah* [[(already)]], *lagi* [[(again)]], and *berhenti* [[(stop)]]. The pair *bahkan … pun* [[(even)]] adds a scalar presupposition, as in the Snapshot: “even Rani” treats Rani as the student least likely to fail."},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Clickbait headlines and social media captions are dense with these triggers precisely because they allow a writer to assert one newsworthy claim while backgrounding another claim that readers may never think to question. Recent headline research identifies this as a form of manipulative rhetoric (Malyuga & Petrosyan, 2024)."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"Entailment, presupposition, and implicature: the cancellability test"},{"type":"table","id":"s3-cancel","header":["Relation","Can it be cancelled?","Under negation"],"rows":[{"cells":["Entailment","No. Cancelling it contradicts the sentence.","Does not survive: negating the sentence removes the guarantee."]},{"cells":["Presupposition","Only by explicit context.","Typically persists."]},{"cells":["Implicature","Yes, and it is context-dependent. Previewed here; covered in Unit 4.","Not tested this way."]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** A social media user might defend a post by saying, “I never said X, I only implied it.” That defense holds only for implicature, since only implicature can be cancelled or reframed without contradiction. Recognizing this lets you judge whether a post’s “just asking questions” defense is linguistically honest, or whether it is evading a presupposition the post actually depends on."},{"type":"anchor","verse":"al-ahzab-33-70","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan sadida","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: a presupposition that misrepresents fact can render a text misleading even when every individual sentence in it is technically true. Recognizing presupposition is also a critical-reading and truth-seeking skill rather than a mere grammatical exercise, which is why this unit pairs qaulan sadida with Cinta Ilmu."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Because presupposition, unlike entailment, allows a sentence to treat an unstated assumption as if it were already agreed fact, practicing qaulan sadida by making that assumption explicit and verifiable becomes an expression of Cinta Ilmu, protecting both the writer’s honesty and the reader’s access to truth."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Presuppositions survive negation, and entailments do not. “Rani stopped coming to class” and “Rani did not stop coming to class” both presuppose that she used to come.","lines":[["Rani finished her assignment.","entails: Rani did some work"],["Rani did not stop coming.","presupposes: she used to come"]]}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"One underlying event, a deadline dispute, and one communicative intent: *to ask whether the deadline was really extended, without presupposing that it was.* Write it in three registers. For each version, name the trigger a careless wording would have used, and show how your wording avoids it while keeping the asserted content verifiable."},{"type":"table","id":"s4-registers","header":["Register","Your version","Trigger a careless wording would have used"],"rows":[{"cells":["Casual chat message",{"input":true},"For example: “since the deadline was already extended once…”\n[[(a change-of-state / iterative trigger)]]"]},{"cells":["Formal email to the lecturer",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Public class-group post",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"task","label":"Reflect","paras":["Which version was hardest to write without a presupposition, and why? Which one would you trust most if you read it half-asleep?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-reflect","prompts":[{"id":"reflect","label":"Your reflection"}]}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Take a real headline or caption from your own social media feed. Retype it rather than submitting a screenshot. Then work through the steps below."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-steps","header":["Step","Your work"],"rows":[{"cells":["1. The text (retyped)",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["2. Asserted content",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["3. Presupposed content, and the trigger",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["4. Negation test",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["5. Question test",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["6. One thing it entails",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["7. Your rewrite, with the presupposition explicit and verifiable",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"adab","label":"Before you hand in · the rubric","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s5-rubric","items":["The entailment–presupposition distinction is accurate.","The negation test is applied correctly.","The rewrite embodies qaulan sadida without becoming unreadable."]}}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Log one digital text in which a specific presupposition trigger can be identified by name. In the *Unit focus* column, name the trigger and state, in your own words, what fact the text assumed rather than argued for. This builds directly on the Unit 2 skill of tracking reference, since presupposition often relies on a definite reference such as “the policy” or “his decision.”"},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"The presupposition trigger, and the fact the text assumed rather than argued","example":{"time":"20:40","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"Study group chat. Friend B wrote to me, retyped.","said":"“Sudah berhenti ikut diskusi grup, ya?” (So you’ve stopped joining the group discussion, then?)","meant":"A gentle complaint: Friend B noticed I had been quiet and wanted me to join again.","focus":"Trigger: “berhenti” (stop), a change-of-state word. The text assumed, rather than argued, that I used to join the discussions regularly."}},{"type":"note","text":"**Unit focus this week:** the presupposition trigger, and the fact the text assumed rather than argued. Exchanges are retyped and anonymized by role, as in Unit 1."},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 4 turns to the Cooperative Principle and implicature, in which meaning arises from flouting a conversational norm rather than from logical necessity or a built-in assumption."}]}],"references":["Beaver, D. I., Geurts, B., & Denlinger, K. (2011). Presupposition. In E. N. Zalta & U. Nodelman (Eds.), *The Stanford encyclopedia of philosophy*. Stanford University. https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/presupposition/ (Substantive revision published 2021)","Birner, B. J. (2013). *Introduction to pragmatics*. Wiley-Blackwell.","Dagan, I., Glickman, O., & Magnini, B. (2006). The PASCAL recognising textual entailment challenge. In J. Quiñonero-Candela, I. Dagan, B. Magnini, & F. d’Alché-Buc (Eds.), *Machine learning challenges: Evaluating predictive uncertainty, visual object classification, and recognising textual entailment* (pp. 177–190). Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/11736790_9","Huang, Y. (2014). *Pragmatics* (2nd ed.). Oxford University Press.","Karttunen, L. (1973). Presuppositions of compound sentences. *Linguistic Inquiry, 4*(2), 169–193.","Levinson, S. C. (1983). *Pragmatics*. Cambridge University Press.","Malyuga, E. N., & Petrosyan, G. O. (2024). Pragmatic presupposition in the headlines of English-language business media as an element of manipulative rhetoric. *Vestnik Tomskogo Gosudarstvennogo Universiteta. Filologiya, (91)*, 104–130. https://doi.org/10.17223/19986645/91/6","Yule, G. (1996). *Pragmatics*. Oxford University Press.","Zeng, X., Abumansour, A. S., & Zubiaga, A. (2021). Automated fact-checking: A survey. *Language and Linguistics Compass, 15*(10), Article e12438. https://doi.org/10.1111/lnc3.12438"]},{"id":"unit04","number":4,"title":"Cooperative Principle and Implicature","titleId":"Prinsip Kerja Sama dan Makna Tersembunyi","lead":"You already know that meaning in digital communication depends on context, and that a message may communicate more than its literal words. Unit 3 looked at what a sentence takes for granted. This unit looks at what a speaker suggests without saying it, and at how the hearer works it out.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Cooperative Principle, Grice’s Maxims, and implicature","qaulan":"Qaulan baligha, deepened from Unit 2. QS. An-Nisa 4:63","pancaCinta":"Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish between explicitly stated meaning and implied meaning (implicature) in digital communication."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Identify how short replies, emojis, punctuation, silence, and indirect expressions can create implied meaning online."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Explain how effective interpretation and expression of implied meaning support Qaulan Baligha, deepened from Unit 2, and Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia."},{"role":"Production","text":"Analyze and produce appropriate implied meanings in casual, academic, and public digital communication while avoiding unnecessary ambiguity or hurtful sarcasm."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Record one anonymized digital exchange in which the intended meaning was not stated directly, and explain the clues used to infer it."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Read this short group-chat exchange, constructed for this module (Indonesian original, with an English gloss). Din and Nur are classmates preparing a presentation."},{"type":"chat","title":"Group chat","sub":"constructed for this module","messages":[{"who":"Din","side":"recv","text":"Guys, jadi ketemuan malam ini buat nyelesaiin presentasinya?","gloss":"Guys, are we meeting tonight to finish the presentation?","time":""},{"who":"Nur","side":"sent","text":"Aku ada kelas jam 8 pagi besok 😭","gloss":"I have an 8 a.m. class tomorrow.","time":""},{"who":"Din","side":"recv","text":"Jadi… malam ini?","gloss":"So… tonight?","time":""},{"who":"Nur","side":"sent","text":"Aku nggak bilang aku bisa 😂","gloss":"I never said I could.","time":""}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 4.1","text":"Din and Nur. Note what Nur does not say."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Answer these four questions before reading on:"]},{"type":"list","style":"number","items":["What did Nur literally say?","Did she actually say “No”?","What does she probably want Din to understand?","What clues helped you reach that interpretation?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-answers","prompts":[{"id":"answers","label":"Your answers"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Compare several digital responses and discuss their possible implied meanings. Complete the last column for each row. The first row is done for you."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-implicature","header":["Situation","Response","Possible implicature"],"rows":[{"example":true,"cells":["“Are you joining us tonight?”","“I have an assignment due tomorrow 😭”","I probably cannot join."]},{"cells":["“Have you submitted the assignment?”","“I’m still working on the final part.”",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["“Do you like my new haircut?”","“Wow, that’s… different 😂”",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["“Can you finish the slides tonight?”","“I’ll try.”",{"input":true}]}]},{"type":"task","label":"Contrast","paras":["What is explicitly said, and what does the reader or hearer infer? Compare the two for each row."]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["Consider when indirectness helps maintain kindness and when it creates unnecessary confusion. Effective communication should balance clarity and consideration."]},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Flouting a maxim is not lying. The speaker still expects you to look for the meaning behind the surface answer. “I’ll try.” can imply doubt, depending on context."}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four ideas for what you just did by instinct. The Din–Nur exchange is the running example."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"The Cooperative Principle"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · Cooperative Principle","paras":["People generally communicate with the expectation that others will contribute meaningfully to an interaction (Grice, 1975)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** A student asks, “Are you joining the meeting tonight?” and a friend replies, “I have an 8 a.m. class tomorrow.” The reply may imply “No, I cannot join.”"},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"The four Gricean maxims"},{"type":"p","text":"Quantity, Quality, Relation, and Manner describe common expectations that help conversations work smoothly."},{"type":"table","id":"s3-maxims","header":["Quantity","Quality","Relation","Manner"],"rows":[{"cells":["Give enough information.","Be truthful.","Be relevant.","Be clear."]}],"strip":true},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Short digital replies such as “Okay,” “Noted,” “Sure,” or “Interesting…” can communicate different meanings depending on context. Indonesian chats add their own, such as “Ya sudah.” [[(All right, then)]], which can read as calm acceptance or as resigned displeasure."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"Implicature"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · implicature","paras":["Meaning that is suggested or inferred rather than directly stated."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** “Your presentation was… interesting” may imply a negative evaluation even though the literal word *interesting* is positive or neutral."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"Flouting a maxim"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · flouting a maxim","paras":["A speaker deliberately appears to break a conversational expectation so that the hearer looks for an additional meaning. This is intentionally not following an expected conversational pattern to communicate another meaning."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Asked “Did you like my presentation?”, someone replies, “The slides looked really professional.” The speaker avoids directly evaluating the presentation."},{"type":"task","label":"Try it · a simple example","paras":["**Rina:** Budi sudah selesai mengerjakan tugasnya? [[(Has Budi finished the assignment?)]]","**Sari:** Dia sudah selesai bagian pendahuluannya. [[(He has finished the introduction.)]]","Identify: what is explicitly stated? What is probably implied? Which maxim helps explain the interpretation?","**Key idea:** what is said is not always everything that is meant."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s3-try","prompts":[{"id":"stated","label":"What is explicitly stated?"},{"id":"implied","label":"What is probably implied?"},{"id":"maxim","label":"Which maxim helps explain the interpretation?"}]},{"type":"anchor","verse":"an-nisa-4-63","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan baligha, deepened from Unit 2","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: Unit 2 asked whether a speaker’s references reach the listener; qaulan baligha, deepened here, asks whether the implied meaning does too. Considering what others are likely to understand from our words is a concrete form of care (Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia)."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Because implicature lets us mean more than we say, practicing qaulan baligha, deepened from Unit 2, means aiming implied meaning so that it reaches the hearer as intended, an expression of Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia, by considering what others are likely to understand from our words, not only what we literally say."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Implicatures can be cancelled; entailments cannot. “Some of the group replied. In fact, all of them did.” is not a contradiction, because “not all” was only implied.","lines":[["Some of the group replied.","implies: not all of them"],["…In fact, all of them did.","cancels that implicature"]]}]},{"id":"practice","tag":"Practice","title":"Literal or Implied?","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A short check before Register Shift. Read each exchange, judge the **last message in the thread**, and use the clues (the question, the relationship, the timing, the wording) to justify your choice."},{"type":"quiz","id":"s-quiz","title":"Literal or implied?","intro":"For each message, decide whether the speaker means what the words say (literal) or something more (implied). If you choose implied, pick the most likely meaning. The messages are constructed for this module. Your score is only for you.","items":[{"id":"q1","kind":"literal","thread":[{"who":"Rina","side":"recv","text":"Kamu sudah baca materi minggu ini?","gloss":"Have you read this week’s material?"},{"who":"Sari","side":"sent","text":"Sudah, semuanya.","gloss":"Yes, all of it."}],"why":"Sari answers exactly what was asked and adds nothing else. There is no clue that she means more than she says, so the meaning is literal. Not every message hides something; over-reading is also a pragmatic mistake."},{"id":"q2","kind":"implied","thread":[{"who":"Andi","side":"recv","text":"Pak, apakah tugas saya sudah diperiksa?","gloss":"Sir, has my assignment been checked?"},{"who":"Lecturer","side":"sent","text":"Saya baru pulang dari luar kota kemarin.","gloss":"I only got back from out of town yesterday."}],"options":[{"t":"Not yet. Please be patient.","ok":true},{"t":"It has been checked and the mark is ready."},{"t":"The lecturer wants to talk about the trip."}],"why":"The reply does not answer the question directly, so Andi works out the link: someone who has just returned from travelling probably has not yet checked the work. Clues: the question asked, the lecturer’s role, and the timing. Andi can read this as an explanation rather than a refusal."},{"id":"q3","kind":"implied","thread":[{"who":"Dewi","side":"recv","text":"Bagus nggak desain slide-ku?","gloss":"Is my slide design good?"},{"who":"Fajar","side":"sent","text":"Fotonya banyak ya.","gloss":"There are a lot of photos, aren’t there."}],"options":[{"t":"He loves the design and the photos."},{"t":"He is just counting the photos."},{"t":"The design may be too crowded, but he does not want to say so directly.","ok":true}],"why":"Dewi asked for an evaluation and received an observation instead, so Fajar seems to avoid answering the question (a possible flout of Relation). The clue is the gap between what was asked and what was said. In a real chat more than one reading is possible, so you would check with a follow-up."},{"id":"q4","kind":"literal","thread":[{"who":"Lecturer","side":"recv","text":"Kuliah besok pukul 08.00 di ruang 3.2.","gloss":"Tomorrow’s class is at 08:00 in room 3.2."}],"why":"An announcement gives the time and place and nothing more. It is clear and complete, so the literal reading is the intended one."},{"id":"q5","kind":"implied","thread":[{"who":"Group leader","side":"recv","text":"Siapa yang bisa bantu menyusun slide?","gloss":"Who can help put the slides together?"},{"who":"Fina","side":"sent","text":"Aku lagi sibuk banget minggu ini sih…","gloss":"I’m really busy this week, you know…"}],"options":[{"t":"She is volunteering to do it this week."},{"t":"She is not volunteering, and is declining gently.","ok":true},{"t":"She is asking the leader about her own schedule."}],"why":"A call for volunteers is answered with a statement about being busy, never with a clear “yes” or “no”. The softening “sih…” suggests she is declining without saying the word. Compare this with the Nur example in Stage 1."},{"id":"q6","kind":"implied","thread":[{"who":"Nur","side":"recv","text":"Aku nunggu dari tadi lho.","gloss":"I’ve been waiting since earlier, you know."},{"who":"Din","side":"sent","text":"Maaf ya, tadi macet.","gloss":"Sorry, there was a traffic jam."},{"who":"Nur","side":"recv","text":"Ya sudah.","gloss":"All right, then."}],"options":[{"t":"She has completely forgotten the waiting and is happy."},{"t":"She accepts the apology, but some irritation remains.","ok":true},{"t":"She wants Din to explain the traffic in more detail."}],"why":"“Ya sudah.” can read as calm acceptance or as resigned displeasure. The earlier complaint and the very short reply are the clues here; without them the same words could be neutral. Context decides, not the words alone."},{"id":"q7","kind":"literal","thread":[{"who":"Din","side":"recv","text":"Kamu bisa kirim filenya sekarang?","gloss":"Can you send the file now?"},{"who":"Nur","side":"sent","text":"Bisa. Aku kirim sekarang.","gloss":"Yes. I’ll send it now."}],"why":"A direct answer to a direct question, with a promise that matches it. Nothing is suggested beyond what is said."},{"id":"q8","kind":"implied","scene":"The group agreed to start at 10:00. Rafi arrives at 10:40.","thread":[{"who":"Bima","side":"recv","text":"Wah, cepat sekali datangnya.","gloss":"Wow, you got here really early."}],"options":[{"t":"He is praising Rafi for being early."},{"t":"He is pointing out, indirectly, that Rafi is late.","ok":true},{"t":"He is asking how Rafi travelled."}],"why":"Bima says something that everyone knows is not true (a flout of Quality), so the hearer looks for the opposite meaning. The clue is the shared knowledge that Rafi arrived forty minutes late. Irony like this can embarrass, and in a chat the tone is missing; a short, plain reminder of the start time would be kinder and clearer."}]},{"type":"note","text":"This practice stage is an addition for the app; it is not part of the printed module."}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Express the same communicative intention, declining an invitation, in three digital registers. Write in Indonesian or your usual chat language, with an English gloss. Here are three examples."},{"type":"table","id":"s4-examples","header":["Register","Message"],"rows":[{"cells":["To a close friend","“Nggak bisa nih 😭 Ada tugas malam ini.”\n[[(Can’t make it, I have an assignment tonight.)]]"]},{"cells":["To a lecturer","“Terima kasih atas undangannya, Pak. Mohon maaf, saya tidak dapat hadir karena ada tenggat tugas malam ini.”\n[[(Thank you for the invitation, Sir. Unfortunately, I will not be able to attend because I have an assignment deadline tonight.)]]"]},{"cells":["To a class group","“Maaf semua, kayaknya aku nggak bisa ikut malam ini karena harus menyelesaikan tugas.”\n[[(Sorry everyone, I don’t think I can join tonight because I need to finish my assignment.)]]"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-own","prompts":[{"id":"friend","label":"Your version: to a close friend"},{"id":"lecturer","label":"Your version: to a lecturer"},{"id":"group","label":"Your version: to a class group"}]},{"type":"task","label":"Discuss","paras":["• How does directness change?","• Where is indirectness acceptable?","• Could “Insyaallah, ya” [[(God willing)]] or “Lihat nanti ya” [[(we’ll see)]] have different meanings depending on the relationship?","• When does being indirect become too unclear?"]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab connection","paras":["The most appropriate message is not necessarily the most formal one. Consider the relationship, context, clarity, and the possible effect on the reader."]}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"**Task: “What Do You Really Mean?”** Create a short digital message that contains an implied meaning. It may be in Indonesian, English, or a mix, as it would really be sent, with an English gloss. A partner then reads it and completes the right-hand column."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-work","header":["","Your work"],"rows":[{"cells":["Your message (with English gloss)",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Partner: 1. What was literally said?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Partner: 2. What was probably meant?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Partner: 3. What clues support the interpretation?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Partner: 4. Which maxim is relevant?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Partner: 5. Could the message be misunderstood?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Your revision, if necessary",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"note","text":"**REVIEW RUBRIC**"},{"type":"table","id":"s5-rubric","header":["Criterion","Look for"],"rows":[{"cells":["Pragmatic interpretation","Correct distinction between literal and implied meaning"]},{"cells":["Context awareness","Appropriate consideration of relationship, situation, and platform"]},{"cells":["Clarity","Intended meaning can reasonably be understood"]},{"cells":["Adab","Message avoids unnecessary embarrassment, insult, or hurt"]},{"cells":["Register control","Language fits the intended audience"]}],"keyCol":true}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Record one real but anonymized digital interaction from the week where the intended meaning was not stated directly. Use the four-column log. In the *Unit focus* column, answer these questions:"},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["What clues helped me infer the meaning?","Which maxim was relevant?","Was the implied meaning helpful or confusing?","Optional: could the sender have expressed the meaning more clearly?"]},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"The clues, the maxim, helped or confusing (and optionally, a clearer version)","example":{"time":"18:05","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"Chat with Friend A about a seminar. Friend A asked first; this is my answer, retyped.","said":"“Besok aku ada kuis.” (Tomorrow I have a quiz.)","meant":"No, I cannot come to the seminar tomorrow.","focus":"Clues: Friend A had asked about tomorrow, and a reason is the usual way to decline. Maxim: Relation (it looks off-topic, but it answers the question). Helpful: we both understood. A clearer version: “Maaf, aku nggak bisa ikut karena ada kuis.” (Sorry, I can’t come because I have a quiz.)"}},{"type":"define","label":"Before you log · protecting other people","paras":["Retype and anonymize messages rather than submitting screenshots containing names, phone numbers, photographs, or other identifying information. In the Exchange column, give the platform and the relationship by role (friend, lecturer, classmate), and use role labels such as Friend A or Lecturer instead of names, because a lecturer or a small class is easy to identify. Retype in the original language, with an English gloss if it is not English."]},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 5 builds on this by asking how speakers use language to perform actions: Speech Acts."}]}],"references":["Grice, H. P. (1975). Logic and conversation. In P. Cole & J. L. Morgan (Eds.), *Syntax and semantics: Vol. 3. Speech acts* (pp. 41–58). Academic Press."]},{"id":"unit05","number":5,"title":"Doing Things with Words","titleId":"Tindak Tutur dalam Ruang Digital (Speech Acts)","subtitle":"Speech Acts in Digital Space","lead":"Units 2 to 4 trained you to recover what a message means beyond its literal words. This unit asks what a message *does*. The object of analysis shifts from information that is communicated to action that is performed: a promise binds, a request obliges, an apology repairs, and a reaction button can accept or dismiss.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Speech acts","qaulan":"Qaulan ma’rufa: good, fitting speech. QS. An-Nisa 4:5","pancaCinta":"Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya (love of Allah and His Messenger)"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish locution, illocution, and perlocution (Austin, 1962); classify acts into Searle’s five types, tell direct from indirect acts, and use felicity conditions to judge whether an act succeeded, misfired, or was insincere (Searle, 1969, 1976)."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Explain how emoji, punctuation, reaction buttons, read receipts, and administrator actions carry, or lose, the force of a message in platform-mediated settings."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Connect felicity conditions to qaulan ma’rufa (speech fitting to the person, relationship, and occasion), and the accountability of one’s word to Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya."},{"role":"Production","text":"Name the act a digital utterance performs and its type, say which felicity condition it satisfies or fails, and rewrite it so the intended act is clear and appropriate, once in a public register and once in a private one."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Log one message or platform action whose force was unclear or was received as a different act, and state in one sentence what it actually did, as opposed to what it said."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The Rani–Dr. Hakim thread continues with a new post. Rani did not resubmit before the deadline set in Unit 2. The day after the classmate’s remark in Unit 3, she writes in the class group. Dr. Hakim reacts with a single “👍” and does not reply in writing."},{"type":"chat","title":"Class group","sub":"constructed for this module","messages":[{"who":"Rani","side":"sent","text":"“Maaf kalau keterlambatan pengumpulan tugas saya mengganggu siapa pun 🙏 Ada yang bisa kasih tahu deadline barunya? Saya janji kumpulkan malam ini.”","gloss":"Sorry if my late submission bothered anyone. Can someone share the new deadline? I promise I will submit tonight.","time":"The next morning"},{"reaction":"👍","by":"Dr. Hakim"}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 5.1","text":"Rani’s post, and the reaction beneath it."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Before judging whether Rani’s message was appropriate, name every act the message performs, and the act performed by Dr. Hakim’s reaction. State what each one commits the sender to."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-acts","prompts":[{"id":"acts","label":"Acts and what they commit the sender to"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complete the table for Rani’s message and for Dr. Hakim’s reaction: the part of the message, its locution, the illocutionary act (with its Searle type), and the likely perlocutionary effect on the reader. The first row is done for you."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-acts","header":["Part of the message","Locution","Illocutionary act (Searle type)","Likely perlocutionary effect"],"rows":[{"example":true,"cells":["“Ada yang bisa kasih tahu deadline barunya?”","Can someone tell me the new deadline?","A request, in the form of a question (directive; indirect)","Everyone may assume someone else will answer."]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}]},{"type":"task","label":"Compare","paras":["Compare “Maaf kalau keterlambatan pengumpulan tugas saya mengganggu siapa pun” [[(Sorry if my late submission bothered anyone)]] with “Saya minta maaf karena keterlambatan pengumpulan tugas saya menghambat kelas” [[(I am sorry that my late submission delayed the class)]]. Which one owns the act and satisfies the sincerity condition?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s2-compare","prompts":[{"id":"which","label":"Your answer"}]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["A conditional apology and a promise made without a realistic plan to keep it are both words that cannot later be honoured. Treating a “👍” as formal permission without confirming it risks a lapse in *tabayyun* (verifying before acting or sharing), because a reaction is an acknowledgement whose force the recipient has not verified."]},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"A thumbs-up can accept, acknowledge, endorse or dismiss. The platform button is the same; the act depends on the situation."}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Rani’s post is the running example throughout."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Locution, illocution, and perlocution"},{"type":"define","label":"Key terms · the three levels of an utterance","paras":["**Locution:** what is literally said. **Illocution:** what the speaker does by saying it. **Perlocution:** what effect it has on the hearer. Only the first is fixed by the words; the second depends on convention and context; the third depends on the hearer (Austin, 1962; Yule, 1996)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** The message “I will send it tonight” has the same locution whether it is a promise, a prediction, or a warning. It also has different perlocutions depending on the reader: one reader feels reassured, another has heard it three times before. On a platform, perlocution is visible to everyone: a reaction count or “seen by 28” appears beneath the act."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"Searle’s five types and felicity conditions"},{"type":"p","text":"Illocutionary acts fall into five families (Searle, 1976):"},{"type":"table","id":"s3-types","header":["Type","What it does","Digital example"],"rows":[{"cells":["Assertives","Stating, claiming","A caption that states or claims something"]},{"cells":["Directives","Requesting, asking, ordering","“Ada yang bisa kirim slide-nya?” [[(Can someone share the slides?)]]"]},{"cells":["Commissives","Promising, offering","“Aku akan update tiap hari!” [[(I will update every day!)]]"]},{"cells":["Expressives","Thanking, apologizing, congratulating","“Maaf kalau ada yang tersinggung” [[(Sorry if anyone was offended)]], a hedged apology"]},{"cells":["Declarations","Acts that change a status by being performed, such as “The meeting is now closed”","An administrator removing a member, closing comments, or pinning a rule"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · felicity conditions","paras":["An act succeeds only if its felicity conditions hold (Searle, 1969): **propositional content** (the content suits the act), **preparatory** (the preconditions are met), **sincerity** (the speaker means it), and **essential** (the utterance counts as an attempt to perform that act)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Public promises in captions and stories, apology posts that hedge (which do not own the act), and thanks posted to a whole group all test sincerity and preparatory conditions in front of an audience. Group administrators perform declarations whenever they remove a member, close comments, or pin a rule, because their position, not their words, gives the act its force."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"Direct and indirect speech acts"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · direct and indirect acts","paras":["An act is **direct** when its form matches its function (a question asks, an imperative orders) and **indirect** when one act is performed by means of another. “Ada yang bisa kirim slide-nya?” [[(Can someone share the slides?)]] is a question in form and a request in function (Searle, 1975)."]},{"type":"table","id":"s3-tests","header":["Two quick tests","How to use them"],"rows":[{"cells":["The “hereby” test","Add “hereby” to a performative sentence: “I hereby apologize.”"]},{"cells":["The purpose test","Ask: “what is this for, and what would count as a satisfactory reply?”"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Group chats are full of indirect directives: “Ada yang punya filenya?” [[(Does anyone have the file?)]], “Alangkah baiknya kalau catatannya diunggah” [[(It would be great if the notes were uploaded)]], “Masih nunggu 🙏” [[(still waiting)]]. The indirectness spares the writer the face threat of ordering, but it also lets everyone in the group assume that someone else will answer, a pattern familiar as “read but not replied.”"},{"type":"p","text":"**Not the same as implicature.** Unlike the implicatures of Unit 4, which depend on reasoning from a flouted maxim (Grice, 1975), conventionalized indirect requests are read almost automatically: through convention and felicity conditions, not through a flouted maxim."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"Force-indicating devices and platform acts"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · force-indicating devices","paras":["Speakers signal which act they are performing with devices such as word order, performative verbs, intonation, and, in writing, punctuation and emoji. In digital text, where intonation is absent, these compensate for the loss of tone. Platform buttons then turn reactions into acts of their own (accepting, endorsing, acknowledging, dismissing)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** A single “Ok.” may be acceptance, resignation, or rebuke, and a “👍” from a lecturer under a late submission may mean permission, acknowledgement, or nothing at all. Automated systems face the same problem: dialogue-act tagging, which labels utterances as statements, questions, requests, and so on, is a standard task in language technology (Stolcke et al., 2000), and every chatbot that decides what a user wants is guessing an illocutionary force."},{"type":"anchor","verse":"an-nisa-4-5","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan ma’rufa","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: the felicity conditions of a speech act are the conditions under which an utterance is fitting to the person, the relationship, and the occasion. A promise or apology is also a word later held to account (QS. Al-Isra 17:34; QS. Ash-Shaff 61:2–3), which is why this unit pairs it with Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Because every speech act changes the social world by obliging, requesting, repairing, or releasing someone, performing it in a form that is appropriate to the hearer and honest about the speaker’s real intention and ability to follow through is the practice of qaulan ma’rufa, and holding oneself to the word one has given is an expression of Cinta Allah dan Rasul-Nya, protecting both the speaker’s integrity and the hearer’s right to rely on what was said."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Acts can misfire. Typing “I hereby fire you” does nothing if you are not the person’s employer, because the felicity conditions are not met. The same words can succeed from someone with the authority to say them."}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"One underlying event, a missed deadline, and two acts to perform: **an apology** and **a request for an extension**. Write them in three versions that differ in directness and form. For each version, mark the act type, whether it is direct or indirect, and which felicity condition a careless version would break (for example, a public promise that the writer is not sure they can keep)."},{"type":"table","id":"s4-registers","header":["Register","Your version","Act type","Direct or indirect?","Felicity condition a careless version would break"],"rows":[{"cells":["Casual chat message to a friend",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Formal email to the lecturer",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Public class-group post",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"task","label":"Reflect","paras":["Which version was hardest to write honestly, and why? What does the public version risk that the private ones do not?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-reflect","prompts":[{"id":"reflect","label":"Your reflection"}]}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Collect five short utterances from your own chats or feeds, retyped and anonymized, and classify each by Searle’s types. Then choose one in which the force is unclear or a felicity condition fails (for example, an insincere apology, an empty promise, or a request disguised as a question)."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-collect","header":["#","Utterance (retyped, anonymized)","Searle type"],"rows":[{"cells":["1",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["2",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["3",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["4",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["5",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}]},{"type":"table","id":"s5-chosen","header":["The one you chose","Your work"],"rows":[{"cells":["Which felicity condition fails, or why is the force unclear?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Rewrite as a public post",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Rewrite as a private message",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"adab","label":"Before you hand in · the rubric","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s5-rubric","items":["The act classification is accurate.","Felicity conditions are used correctly to explain the failure.","The direct/indirect distinction is applied correctly.","The rewrite embodies qaulan ma’rufa without turning into a stiff formula."]}}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Log one digital message or platform action (including an emoji, a reaction, or a read receipt) whose force was unclear, or was received as a different act from the one intended. In the *Unit focus* column, state in one sentence what it actually did, as opposed to what it said. This builds on the “what was said / what I think was meant” log by moving from the meaning that is implied to the action that is performed: ask not only “what was meant?” but also “what did this message do?”"},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"What the message actually did, as opposed to what it said","example":{"time":"21:10","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"Chat with Classmate C. He wrote to me, retyped.","said":"“Bisa kirim filenya sekarang?” (Can you send the file now?)","meant":"A request to send the file, with some urgency. It was not a question about whether I am able to.","focus":"It did not ask about my ability. It performed a request, and the word “sekarang” (now) added pressure that the question form hid."}},{"type":"note","text":"**Unit focus this week:** what the message actually did, including reactions and read receipts. Exchanges are retyped and anonymized by role, as in Unit 1."},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 6 asks how speakers soften, intensify, or avoid acts that threaten the other person’s face, such as refusals, criticisms, and requests."}]}],"references":["Austin, J. L. (1962). *How to do things with words*. Clarendon Press.","Birner, B. J. (2013). *Introduction to pragmatics*. Wiley-Blackwell.","Grice, H. P. (1975). Logic and conversation. In P. Cole & J. L. Morgan (Eds.), *Syntax and semantics: Vol. 3. Speech acts* (pp. 41–58). Academic Press.","Huang, Y. (2014). *Pragmatics* (2nd ed.). Oxford University Press.","Levinson, S. C. (1983). *Pragmatics*. Cambridge University Press.","Searle, J. R. (1969). *Speech acts: An essay in the philosophy of language*. Cambridge University Press.","Searle, J. R. (1975). Indirect speech acts. In P. Cole & J. L. Morgan (Eds.), *Syntax and semantics: Vol. 3. Speech acts* (pp. 59–82). Academic Press.","Searle, J. R. (1976). A classification of illocutionary acts. *Language in Society, 5*(1), 1–23.","Stolcke, A., Ries, K., Coccaro, N., Shriberg, E., Bates, R., Jurafsky, D., Taylor, P., Martin, R., Van Ess-Dykema, C., & Meteer, M. (2000). Dialogue act modeling for automatic tagging and recognition of conversational speech. *Computational Linguistics, 26*(3), 339–373.","Yule, G. (1996). *Pragmatics*. Oxford University Press."]},{"id":"unit06","number":6,"title":"Politeness and Impoliteness across Cultures","titleId":"Kesantunan dan Ketidaksantunan Lintas Budaya: Muka, Strategi Bertutur, dan Ketersinggungan dalam Interaksi Digital","subtitle":"Face, Facework, and Offense in Digital Interaction","lead":"Unit 5 asked what a message *does*. This unit asks what it puts at risk: every request, refusal, or criticism affects the standing of both people involved, and cultures calibrate that risk differently. In text, small choices (an address term, an opener, an emoji, a silence) carry the weight.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Face, politeness, and impoliteness","qaulan":"Qaulan layyina (QS. Taha 20:44) and qaulan maysura (QS. Al-Isra 17:28), with ma’rufa in support","pancaCinta":"Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia (love of self and fellow human beings)"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish politeness as a face-redressive strategy (Brown & Levinson, 1987) from *tatakrama*, culturally agreed etiquette (Mislikhah, 2014), and from impoliteness, behavior that attacks face (Culpeper, 1996, 2011)."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Identify how text-only, asynchronous, public interaction reshapes face-threat: mitigation rests on address terms, openers, emoji, and punctuation, and silence becomes interpretable."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Explain how face-work, qaulan layyina and qaulan maysura (with ma’rufa in support), and Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia frame respectful speech as care for others and self-respect."},{"role":"Production","text":"Realize one intent in three registers and two languages, justify each choice by distance, power, and imposition, and repair an impolite post so that it respects face and stays clear."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Log one exchange where politeness norms diverged or offense arose: the norm at stake, intended versus perceived effect, the strategy, and one repair."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A student writes to a lecturer about meeting on campus. The Indonesian exchange is adapted from a spoken example in Mislikhah (2014) and anonymized. The English version below it is constructed to show what happens when the same pattern is carried into another language."},{"type":"chat","title":"Messages to a lecturer","sub":"adapted from Mislikhah (2014)","messages":[{"who":"Student","side":"sent","text":"Halo, Anda sekarang ada di mana?","gloss":"Hello, where are you now?","time":""},{"who":"Student","side":"sent","text":"Jam berapa Anda ke kampus?","gloss":"What time are you coming to campus?","time":""},{"who":"Student","side":"sent","text":"ya udah, kalau gitu saya tunggu","gloss":"All right then, I’ll wait.","time":""}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 6.1","text":"The Indonesian exchange. The information requested is clear."},{"type":"chat","title":"Messages to an international lecturer","sub":"constructed for this module","messages":[{"who":"Student","side":"sent","text":"Where are you now? What time do you come to campus? OK, I wait.","gloss":"The same pattern, written in English.","time":""}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 6.2","text":"The English parallel."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Before deciding who is at fault, answer three questions. What does the student ask for in each version, and is it clear? What might a lecturer expect that is missing? Would the Indonesian and the English version be read the same way?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-missing","prompts":[{"id":"missing","label":"What is missing, and how each version might be read"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Below are three versions of one request to a lecturer for a consultation. Rank them for politeness (1 for the most polite) and name the strategy each one uses."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-rank","header":["Version","Message","Politeness rank","Strategy"],"rows":[{"cells":["a","“Selamat siang, Bapak. Mohon maaf mengganggu waktunya. Apakah Bapak berkenan memberi saya waktu konsultasi besok?” [[(Good afternoon, Sir. Sorry to disturb you. Would you be willing to give me a consultation slot tomorrow?)]]",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["b","“Saya mau konsultasi besok. Jam berapa bisa?” [[(I want a consultation tomorrow. What time is possible?)]]",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["c","“Give me a consultation time tomorrow.”",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"Then compare the address-term scale in Indonesian with the options in English."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-scale","header":["Language","Address-term scale"],"rows":[{"cells":["Indonesian (Mislikhah, 2014)","engkau · Saudara · Anda · Bapak/Ibu"]},{"cells":["English","first name · Mr/Ms · Dr · Professor"]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"task","label":"Compare","paras":["Where on each scale would you place a message to your own lecturer? Which opener or closing (greeting, apology, thanks) would you add, and why?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s2-compare","prompts":[{"id":"compare","label":"Your answer"}]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["Address terms and closings are visible expressions of *hormat*, respect toward teachers. They make that respect observable in the language itself, before anyone judges the content of the request."]}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Face, face-threatening acts, and weightiness"},{"type":"define","label":"Key terms · face and face-threatening acts","paras":["**Positive face** is the want to be approved of; **negative face** is the want to be unimpeded. Everyone claims both. A **face-threatening act (FTA)**, such as a request, refusal, or criticism, puts one at risk. The risk, or **weightiness**, grows with **social distance**, the **power** difference between the two people, and the **degree of imposition** of the act (Brown & Levinson, 1987)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** A WhatsApp request to a lecturer is a heavy FTA: address terms and softeners show its weight, and read receipts make silence part of the imposition."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"Politeness strategies and cultural calibration"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · two senses of politeness","paras":["As a **strategy**, politeness redresses a threat to face (Brown & Levinson, 1987). As **etiquette** (*tatakrama*), it is what a community agrees is proper (Mislikhah, 2014). **Impoliteness** attacks face and is intended or perceived as such (Culpeper, 1996, 2011)."]},{"type":"p","text":"Before an FTA, a speaker chooses among five strategies (Brown & Levinson, 1987). Culpeper (1996) describes a mirror set of impoliteness superstrategies, although mock politeness is not simply the counterpart of off-record politeness (Culpeper, 2005)."},{"type":"table","id":"s3-strategies","header":["Politeness strategy","Digital example","Impoliteness strategy","Digital example"],"rows":[{"cells":["Bald on record","“Send me the file.”","Bald on record","“Your post is useless.”"]},{"cells":["Positive politeness","“Hi! Could you send the file? Your notes are the clearest.”","Positive","Name-calling, exclusion: “Nobody asked you.”"]},{"cells":["Negative politeness","“Sorry to bother you, could you send the file?”","Negative","Ridicule or condescension: “Let me explain it slowly.”"]},{"cells":["Off-record","“I still don’t have the file…”","Sarcasm or mock politeness","“Great job 👏” about a late, careless submission."]},{"cells":["Not performing the FTA","The request is never made.","Withhold politeness","Deliberately leaving a message unanswered."]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** What counts as appropriate depends on the culture. Indonesian messages often rely on honorific address (Bapak/Ibu), apologetic openers, and indirectness, the *tidak terus terang* (not straightforward) orientation noted by Mislikhah (2014). English e-mail conventions favor explicit request framing with conventional mitigators. Carried into the other language, each norm can be misread, as excessive deference or as abruptness."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"Impoliteness, context, and perception"},{"type":"p","text":"Whether an utterance offends depends on the context, the speaker’s intention, and the hearer’s perception (Culpeper, 2011). For Indonesian examples, Mislikhah (2014) links impoliteness to blunt criticism in harsh words, emotional speech, defensiveness about one’s own opinion, cornering the addressee, and accusation based on suspicion, and also to ignorance of norms or transfer from the first language."},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Anonymity and an unseen audience can intensify blunt criticism or accusation. The same joke may read as solidarity in a close group chat but as offense in a public thread or an intercultural exchange. Deliberately roundabout or insincere deference can itself be impolite (Mislikhah, 2014), so clarity still matters."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"Qur’anic anchors"},{"type":"p","text":"Two principles guide the choices in this unit: qaulan layyina and qaulan maysura."},{"type":"p","text":"**Supporting links.** Qaulan ma’rufa (QS. An-Nisa 4:5, Unit 5) supports both: speech must also fit the person and the occasion. Qaulan sadida, truthful speech, links to the maxim of quality (Unit 4). Honorific choice is adab, *hormat* toward teachers, not a qaulan pairing."},{"type":"anchor","verse":"taha-20-44","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan layyina","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: layyina is gentle speech, which parallels the mitigation of face-threatening acts. The verse also names its aim: that the hearer may become aware."]},{"type":"anchor","verse":"al-isra-17-28","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan maysura","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: maysura is easy, gentle speech, and it parallels the softened refusal. In the Kemenag translation the speaker is unable to help and has to turn away; the verse asks that the refusal still be given in gentle words."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Because every utterance can protect or damage the face of both speaker and hearer, calibrating strategies to each culture’s norms is a way of speaking layyina and maysura, with ma’rufa in support, that enacts care for others and respect for oneself (Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia), whereas impoliteness shows what follows when that care is withheld or turned into attack."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"A request is called a face-threatening act because it imposes on the other person’s freedom to act. The more distance, power and imposition involved, the more care it needs."}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Choose one intent: declining an invitation, requesting an extension, or giving critical feedback. Express it to a lecturer, to a peer group chat, and in a public comment, in Indonesian and in English. For each version, name the strategy and justify it by social distance, power, and imposition."},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-intent","prompts":[{"id":"intent","label":"My intent"}]},{"type":"table","id":"s4-registers","header":["Register","Indonesian","English","Strategy, with distance, power, imposition"],"rows":[{"cells":["To a lecturer",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["To a peer group chat",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["In a public comment",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"task","label":"Reflect","paras":["Which register changed your wording the most, and in which language were you more direct? Where would deliberate indirectness have been unclear instead of kind?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-reflect","prompts":[{"id":"reflect","label":"Your reflection"}]},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Particles such as *ya*, *dong* and *sih* in informal Indonesian chat can soften or sharpen a message. Their effect depends on the relationship and the tone, so they are not safe to use with everyone."}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Your product has two parts: the three-register message set from Stage 4, revised, and a repair of one impolite online comment that your instructor supplies. Retype the comment below, then repair it."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-repair","header":["Repair task","Your work"],"rows":[{"cells":["The comment (retyped)",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["The face it attacks, and the Culpeper superstrategy",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Intended and perceived effect",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Your repair: respectful of face, and still clear",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["The qaulan principle that guides the repair, and why",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"adab","label":"Before you hand in · the rubric","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s5-rubric","items":["The FTA and the strategy are correctly identified.","Each choice is justified by social distance, power, and imposition.","Address terms and degree of directness fit the culture.","The wording reflects qaulan layyina and maysura, with ma’rufa in support.","The message stays clear: roundabout or insincere deference can itself be impolite.","The language is accurate."]}}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Log one digital exchange in which politeness norms diverged (across languages, generations, or status) or offense arose. In *What I think was meant*, name the cultural norm at stake. In the *Unit focus* column, record the intended versus the perceived effect, the strategy label (Brown and Levinson, or Culpeper), and one repair option."},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"Intended versus perceived effect, the strategy label, and one repair option","example":{"time":"07:50","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"A classmate wrote to Lecturer B; I saw it because I am in the class group, retyped and anonymized.","said":"“Bu, nilai saya kok segini? Tolong direvisi.” (Ma’am, why is my grade like this? Please revise it.)","meant":"Norm at stake: showing respect to a lecturer with a greeting, an apology and softening words.","focus":"Intended: ask for a re-check of the grade. Perceived: blunt and demanding. Strategy: bald on-record, with no redress (Brown and Levinson). Repair: “Selamat pagi, Bu. Mohon maaf mengganggu. Bolehkah saya menanyakan nilai saya?” (Good morning, ma’am. Sorry to disturb you. May I ask about my grade?)"}},{"type":"note","text":"**Unit focus this week:** intended versus perceived effect, the strategy label, and one repair option. Retype and anonymize each exchange by role (Friend A, Lecturer); never submit screenshots. Class work is not research."},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 7 turns this vocabulary into a method: you will use it to code real exchanges from your own Field Journal."}]}],"references":["Brown, P., & Levinson, S. C. (1978). Universals in language usage: Politeness phenomena. In E. N. Goody (Ed.), *Questions and politeness: Strategies in social interaction* (pp. 56–311). Cambridge University Press.","Brown, P., & Levinson, S. C. (1987). *Politeness: Some universals in language usage*. Cambridge University Press.","Culpeper, J. (1996). Towards an anatomy of impoliteness. *Journal of Pragmatics, 25*(3), 349–367.","Culpeper, J. (2005). Impoliteness and entertainment in the television quiz show: The Weakest Link. *Journal of Politeness Research, 1*(1), 35–72.","Culpeper, J. (2011). *Impoliteness: Using language to cause offence*. Cambridge University Press.","Mislikhah, S. (2014). Kesantunan berbahasa. *Ar-Raniry: International Journal of Islamic Studies, 1*(2), 285–296."]},{"id":"unit07","number":7,"title":"Discourse and Pragmatic Research","titleId":"Wacana dan Penelitian Pragmatik: dari Jurnal Lapangan Digital menjadi Data yang Dapat Dianalisis","subtitle":"From Digital Field Journals to Analyzable Data","lead":"Until now you have used pragmatic concepts to read messages. This unit turns your Field Journal into a dataset and asks what makes a message usable as evidence: its context, a question it can answer, a framework to code it, and respect for the person who wrote it.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Pragmatic analysis as a research method","qaulan":"Qaulan sadida (QS. Al-Ahzab 33:70), with baligha (QS. An-Nisa 4:63) in support","pancaCinta":"Cinta Ilmu (love of knowledge), with Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia in support"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish pragmatic analysis, which explains language use in its speech situation and works on the pragmatic unit (illocution plus proposition), from grammatical and semantic analysis of abstract sentences (Wiryotinoyo, 2006), and elicited data from naturally occurring data."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Identify what makes digital interaction usable as data and what endangers it: logged threads carry platform, position, and timing, while a screenshot without context invites misreading and mixed audiences raise ethical questions."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Explain how method, qaulan sadida and baligha, and Cinta Ilmu with Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia define responsible inquiry: accurate reporting, and care for the people whose words become data."},{"role":"Production","text":"Design and report a mini pragmatic study: a question, a data source, a coding framework, context variables, five to eight coded excerpts, and one limitation."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Record this week’s entry as a research record, with the verbatim utterance and a one-line candidate research question."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Two retyped messages, stripped of platform, thread, and names. The pair comes from a study of Palembangnese directives (Afriani, 2019). Your instructor may also share a pooled set of five to eight excerpts from the class’s own Field Journals, each shared with the writer’s agreement and anonymized by role."},{"type":"chat","title":"Two messages, no context","sub":"no platform, no thread, no names","messages":[{"who":"Message 1","side":"recv","text":"Oi, minjem catetan","gloss":"Hey, lend me your notes.","time":""},{"who":"Message 2","side":"recv","text":"Pak Prof boleh aku minjem buku","gloss":"Prof, may I borrow the book?","time":""}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 7.1","text":"The same kind of act, with nothing else to go on."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["Using only the words, code each message: which speech act is it, and which Brown and Levinson strategy does it use? Then your instructor tells you who wrote to whom. Recode both, and note what changed and which piece of context changed it."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-coding","prompts":[{"id":"coding","label":"First coding, and the recoding with context"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Four designs could study the same topic. The notes below come from studies of Palembangnese and Indonesian directives (Afriani, 2019) and of criticizing in English as a lingua franca (El-Dakhs et al., 2019). Complete the grid."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-designs","header":["Design","Data type","What the studies say","Authenticity and control","Ethics, and the question it fits"],"rows":[{"cells":["Written discourse completion task","Elicited","Allows spontaneous responses without face-to-face pressure (Afriani, 2019).",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Role-play","Elicited","Raises authenticity, but is still less authentic than ethnographic data (El-Dakhs et al., 2019).",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Interview","Elicited","Reveals the reasons behind choices (Afriani, 2019).",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Field Journal log","Naturally occurring","Carries platform, thread position, and timing.",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"task","label":"Compare","paras":["Which design would you choose to study how students refuse a lecturer’s invitation? What would you gain with it, and what would you give up?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s2-compare","prompts":[{"id":"compare","label":"Your answer"}]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["Honest reporting and the protection of participants’ face. A study that exposes or ridicules the people behind the data repeats the impoliteness it analyzes."]}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This stage teaches a method, not a new construct."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Speech situation and context"},{"type":"define","label":"Key terms · speech situation and context","paras":["A **speech situation** has seven elements: speaker and addressee, context, purpose, speech act, utterance, time, and place. **Context** is the physical and social environment of an utterance together with the background knowledge the participants share, and it is what makes interpreting the force of an utterance possible (Wiryotinoyo, 2006, drawing on Leech)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Platform, thread position, timestamps, emoji, audience visibility, and the shared knowledge of a group chat are all context. A screenshot of one message without its thread can be coded very differently from the same message with its context restored."},{"type":"p","text":"**Try it.** Restore the context for the Unit 6 lecturer call (“Halo, Anda sekarang ada di mana?” [[(Hello, where are you now?)]]) by completing the checklist."},{"type":"table","id":"s3-situation","header":["Element","The Unit 6 lecturer call","Element","The Unit 6 lecturer call"],"rows":[{"cells":["Speaker and addressee",{"input":true},"Speech act",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Context",{"input":true},"Utterance",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Purpose",{"input":true},"Time",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Place",{"input":true},"",""]}],"keyCols":[0,2]},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"The pragmatic unit and heuristic analysis"},{"type":"define","label":"Key terms · pragmatic unit and heuristic analysis","paras":["The **pragmatic unit** combines an illocution with a proposition. In **heuristic analysis** you hypothesize the force of an utterance, test the hypothesis against the Gricean maxims and the contextual evidence, and, if a maxim is flouted, revise it until an implicature is identified. Such interpretations remain probabilistic (Wiryotinoyo, 2006)."]},{"type":"table","id":"s3-heuristic","header":["1 · Hypothesize","2 · Test","3 · Revise","4 · Identify"],"rows":[{"cells":["“Deadline-nya besok jam 8 ya.” [[(The deadline is tomorrow at 8.)]] Hypothesis: the writer is informing.","Everyone in the group already knows the deadline, so the message adds nothing new: a maxim (quantity) looks flouted.","Perhaps the force is not informing but reminding, even urging.","Implicature: “Please submit before then.” This remains probable, not certain."]}]},{"type":"note","text":"Worked example, constructed for this module."},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Short chat messages routinely carry implicature, for example an informative-looking reminder that functions as a request. Test your first hypothesis against the thread and revise it, instead of coding the surface form."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"From question to limitation"},{"type":"p","text":"A study is a chain: a **question** that pragmatic analysis can answer; a **data source** and design; a **framework** to code the data; the **coding** itself; and a stated **limitation**. Elicitation shapes what can be claimed. Written discourse completion tasks allow spontaneous responses without face-to-face pressure, and interviews reveal the reasons behind choices (Afriani, 2019). Role-plays raise authenticity but remain less authentic than ethnographic data (El-Dakhs et al., 2019). Sociopragmatic variables (power, distance, imposition) structure the scenarios."},{"type":"p","text":"Studies adapt an established scheme to their speech act, code the utterances, and report frequencies in context. The two studies below do this in different ways."},{"type":"table","id":"s3-studies","header":["Study","Framework","Result","Note"],"rows":[{"cells":["Afriani (2019)","Brown and Levinson’s five strategies, applied to Palembangnese directives","Positive politeness in 88 of 179 utterances","A second coder checks bias."]},{"cells":["El-Dakhs et al. (2019)","An adapted criticism scheme: direct and indirect strategies, plus modifiers","Indirect strategies at 53.3%","Situation severity is a decisive factor."]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Coding can be done in qualitative software or in a simple sheet, with a second coder to check bias (Afriani, 2019). Digital cues such as emoji or softening punctuation may be proposed as additional modifiers, provided the adaptation is stated and justified."},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · research ethics in this module","paras":["Retype and anonymize every exchange, use only what its writer has agreed to share (consent by use), and remember that **class work is not research**: publishing anything from student data needs institutional ethics clearance first."]},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"Qur’anic anchors"},{"type":"p","text":"Qaulan sadida is the primary pairing for this unit, and qaulan baligha, from Unit 2, supports it."},{"type":"anchor","verse":"al-ahzab-33-70","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan sadida","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: sadida is truthful, correct speech, and it parallels accuracy and honesty in handling and reporting data."]},{"type":"anchor","verse":"an-nisa-4-63","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan baligha","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: baligha is effective, well-aimed speech, and it parallels precise, well-targeted academic writing."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Pragmatic research turns everyday utterances into evidence by describing language in its context, and doing so truthfully and precisely (qaulan sadida and baligha) while protecting the dignity of the people whose words are analyzed (Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia) is how love of knowledge (Cinta Ilmu) becomes disciplined practice."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"In pragmatic analysis, a reading is a hypothesis, not a proof. Two careful analysts can read the same exchange differently and both be reasonable, as long as each shows the contextual evidence."}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Report one finding, in one sentence, in three registers: an analytic memo to your lecturer or supervisor, a results paragraph in scientific English, and a plain-language, anonymized summary for participants or a public post. For each, justify the differences in directness, detail, and hedging."},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-finding","prompts":[{"id":"finding","label":"My finding, in one sentence"}]},{"type":"table","id":"s4-registers","header":["Register","Your version","Directness, detail, hedging"],"rows":[{"cells":["Analytic memo to the lecturer",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Results paragraph in scientific English",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Plain-language summary for participants",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"task","label":"Reflect","paras":["Which version was hardest to keep accurate? What did you leave out of the plain-language summary, and did leaving it out distort the finding?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-reflect","prompts":[{"id":"reflect","label":"Your reflection"}]}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Your product has two parts: a one-page mini-study plan and a coding table of five to eight excerpts, including one worked heuristic analysis. Retype excerpts in their original language with an English gloss. Write the results paragraph in English."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-plan","header":["Mini-study plan","Your plan"],"rows":[{"cells":["Research question",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Data source and consent",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Framework, and why",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Context variables to document",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"table","id":"s5-coding","header":["#","Excerpt (retyped, with gloss)","Context documented","Code"],"rows":[{"cells":["1",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["2",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["3",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["4",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["5",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["6",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["7",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["8",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}]},{"type":"table","id":"s5-heuristic","header":["Hypothesis","Test against maxims and context","Revision","Implicature"],"rows":[{"cells":[{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"tall":true},{"type":"adab","label":"Before you hand in · the rubric","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s5-rubric","items":["The research question can be answered by pragmatic analysis.","Context is documented through the speech-situation elements.","The coding scheme is explicit and consistently applied.","The heuristic test of at least one implicature is visible.","The fit between design and data is stated, with one limitation.","Anonymization and face-respecting reporting are in place.","The English is accurate and precise (qaulan sadida and baligha)."]}}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This week the *Unit focus* column holds a research record: speaker and addressee, platform or setting, purpose, speech act, time, and consent status. Put the verbatim, anonymized utterance in *What was said*, and add a one-line candidate research question with the framework that could code it, so that the entry can later be coded."},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"The research record: speaker and addressee, platform or setting, purpose, speech act, time, consent status; plus a candidate research question and framework","labels":{"said":"What was said (verbatim)","focus":"Unit focus (research record)"},"example":{"time":"13:20","platform":"WhatsApp","ex":"Class group chat. Student A to the whole group, retyped and anonymized.","said":"“Besok kumpul jam 8 ya, jangan telat.” (Let’s gather at 8 tomorrow, don’t be late.)","meant":"A reminder with a mild warning to be on time.","focus":"Research record: speaker Student A; addressee: the class group; setting: WhatsApp class group; purpose: remind and warn; speech act: directive; time: 13:20; consent status: class exercise, not research data, nobody identifiable. Candidate question: How do students soften directives in class group chats? Framework: Brown and Levinson."}},{"type":"note","text":"**Unit focus this week:** the research record, plus a candidate research question and framework. The research record applies to this week only. Retype and anonymize by role; never submit screenshots."},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 8 reuses this vocabulary and procedure to design a mini digital-pragmatics lesson of your own."}]}],"references":["Afriani, S. H. (2019). Linguistic politeness in Palembangnese directives in Indonesia and its implications for university teaching and learning. *Advances in Social Science, Education and Humanities Research, 254*, 227–233. Atlantis Press.","Brown, P., & Levinson, S. C. (1987). *Politeness: Some universals in language usage*. Cambridge University Press.","Culpeper, J. (1996). Towards an anatomy of impoliteness. *Journal of Pragmatics, 25*(3), 349–367.","Culpeper, J. (2011). *Impoliteness: Using language to cause offence*. Cambridge University Press.","El-Dakhs, D. A. S., Ambreen, F., Zaheer, M., & Gusarova, Y. (2019). A pragmatic analysis of the speech act of criticizing in university teacher-student talk: The case of English as a lingua franca. *Pragmatics, 29*(4), 493–520. https://doi.org/10.1075/prag.18028.eld","Wiryotinoyo, M. (2006). Analisis pragmatik dalam penelitian penggunaan bahasa. *Bahasa dan Seni, 34*(2), 153–163."]},{"id":"unit08","number":8,"title":"Pragmatics in Language Teaching","titleId":"Pragmatik dalam Pengajaran Bahasa","lead":"Unit 7 taught you to treat your own journal as data. This unit turns the same analytic habit toward a teaching question: not only “what does this exchange show?” but “how would I teach someone else to notice it?” Teaching a concept well also means correcting a learner without shaming them.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Pragmatics in language teaching","qaulan":"Qaulan karima: good speech, offered in a mentoring role. QS. Al-Isra 17:23","pancaCinta":"Cinta Ilmu, with Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia in support"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Distinguish pragmalinguistic failure (the wrong form for a speech act) from sociopragmatic failure (misjudging distance, power, and imposition), and both from ordinary grammatical error (Thomas, 1983)."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Explain how digital tools, from searchable chat logs to AI chatbots, can be rich material for noticing pragmatic patterns or an imperfect model of them, and why chatbot output needs a checking step (Díaz-Muñoz & Stecca, 2026)."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Connect qaulan karima to correcting a learner’s pragmatic error without shaming the person, Cinta Ilmu to evidence-based method, and Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia to compassion for a learner still developing."},{"role":"Production","text":"Design a short mini-lesson that teaches one concept from Units 2 to 7 to digital-native peers, with your own Digital Snapshot and an explicit adab connection."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Log one moment in designing the lesson when explaining the concept exposed a gap in your own understanding, and how you closed it."}],"stages":[{"id":"s1","tag":"Stage 1","title":"Digital Snapshot","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An Indonesian student, writing in English, emails a professor about a late assignment. Below it is what the same student would probably have written in Indonesian. Both messages are constructed for this module."},{"type":"chat","title":"Email to a professor","sub":"in English","messages":[{"who":"Student","side":"sent","text":"I need the extension. Send me the new deadline.","gloss":"Grammatically correct English.","time":""}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 8.1","text":"The English email."},{"type":"chat","title":"The same student, in Indonesian","sub":"probable wording","messages":[{"who":"Student","side":"sent","text":"Pak, saya mohon perpanjangan waktu. Mohon kabari tenggat barunya.","gloss":"Sir, I request an extension of time. Please let me know the new deadline.","time":""}]},{"type":"caption","label":"Figure 8.2","text":"The Indonesian counterpart."},{"type":"task","label":"Your task","paras":["The English email is grammatically correct, yet it reads as a demand rather than a request. Is this a grammar problem, or something else? Write your diagnosis before you read on."]},{"type":"responses","id":"s1-diagnosis","prompts":[{"id":"diagnosis","label":"My diagnosis"}]}]},{"id":"s2","tag":"Stage 2","title":"Notice & Compare","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Sort the problem in the English email using Thomas’s (1983) two categories. Complete the grid: what each part asserts, what the student probably intended, and the type of failure (pragmalinguistic, sociopragmatic, or none)."},{"type":"table","id":"s2-failure","header":["Part of the email","What it asserts","What the student probably intended","Failure type"],"rows":[{"cells":["“I need the extension.”",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["“Send me the new deadline.”",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["The email as a whole",{"input":true},{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"task","label":"Compare","paras":["The Indonesian version has an address term (*Pak*), *mohon* (a formal “please”), and an indirect request. Which of these does the English version lack? Did the student need a different form (pragmalinguistic), or a different judgment of the relationship (sociopragmatic)?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s2-compare","prompts":[{"id":"compare","label":"Your answer"}]},{"type":"adab","label":"The adab angle","paras":["If the professor silently judged the student as rude, instead of gently naming the issue, was that a missed opportunity for *qaulan karima*?"]}]},{"id":"s3","tag":"Stage 3","title":"Concept Input","blocks":[{"type":"h2","num":"3.1","title":"Pragmatic failure"},{"type":"define","label":"Key terms · pragmalinguistic and sociopragmatic failure","paras":["**Pragmalinguistic failure** is choosing the wrong linguistic form for an intended speech act, for example “Give me the file” instead of “Could I have the file, please?” **Sociopragmatic failure** is misjudging the social distance or power that should govern the choice, for example treating a professor as casually as a classmate (Thomas, 1983). Neither is a grammatical error: a sentence can be entirely grammatical and still fail."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** A perfectly grammatical email request from an Indonesian student writing in English can still read as rude to a professor. Sorting such cross-cultural exchanges into the two kinds of failure gives a future teacher a diagnostic vocabulary sharper than “impolite.”"},{"type":"h2","num":"3.2","title":"Teachability of pragmatics"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · instructed pragmatics","paras":["Research asks whether pragmatic competence can be taught, and which methods work best. The evidence favors instructed learners, for example those explicitly taught the softener “I was wondering if…”, over learners left to acquire the pattern through exposure alone (Taguchi, 2015)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Digital text suits explicit instruction because, unlike fleeting speech, a chat log or comment thread can be paused, annotated, and reread. The Notice & Compare tables of this module are ready-made consciousness-raising material."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.3","title":"Noticing and metapragmatic awareness"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · the noticing hypothesis","paras":["Learners are more likely to acquire a pragmatic pattern once they consciously notice it and can talk about it, for example by naming how the word “just” softens a request, than by being exposed to it alone (the noticing hypothesis, discussed in Taguchi, 2015)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** Highlighting a presupposition trigger in a screenshot, or annotating why a headline’s implicature works, is a small act of noticing that digital text supports easily and a printed textbook dialogue does not."},{"type":"h2","num":"3.4","title":"AI as teaching resource and teaching risk"},{"type":"define","label":"Key term · checking AI-generated practice material","paras":["Corpora, chatbots, and generative AI can supply varied, on-demand practice input. A chatbot’s pragmatic output is not always reliable, so using it for practice should include a step in which the output is checked against the pattern being taught (Díaz-Muñoz & Stecca, 2026)."]},{"type":"p","text":"**The digital hook.** A teacher can ask a chatbot for the same complaint at several levels of formality, but must first check that each version models the target pattern, not a flattened or inconsistent one: *tabayyun*, verifying before acting or sharing, applied to one’s own teaching materials."},{"type":"task","label":"Check before you use it","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s3-check","items":["Each version models the target pattern.","The registers really differ; none is flattened.","The versions are consistent with one another."]}},{"type":"h2","num":"3.5","title":"Qur’anic anchor"},{"type":"anchor","verse":"al-isra-17-23","label":"Qur’anic anchor · Qaulan karima","paras":["Why it belongs in this unit: karima, in this module, is good speech offered in a mentoring role (the Kemenag translation reads “perkataan yang baik”). Correcting a learner’s pragmatic error without shaming the person is itself teaching pragmatics well."]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Because teaching pragmatics well means diagnosing pragmatic failure and correcting it without shaming the person who made it, practicing qaulan karima toward learners is what allows Cinta Ilmu, rigorous and evidence-based teaching, and Cinta Diri dan Sesama Manusia, compassion for the learner’s dignity, to operate together rather than in tension."},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"Even advanced learners can sound abrupt in email when they use a bare imperative. Adding a softener such as “could”, “would you mind” or “I was wondering if” is a pragmalinguistic choice, not a grammar rule.","lines":[["Send me the file.","too direct for a lecturer"],["Could you please send me the file?","softened request"]]}]},{"id":"s4","tag":"Stage 4","title":"Register Shift","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"You do not produce register variants here. Instead, examine how one teaching point, how to soften a request, must be explained differently to three audiences. What changes is not the content but the explanatory register you must adopt."},{"type":"table","id":"s4-audiences","header":["Learner audience","How you would explain it","What changes, and what stays the same"],"rows":[{"cells":["Young digital-native beginners",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Adult intermediate learners",{"input":true},{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Advanced learners preparing to study abroad",{"input":true},{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"task","label":"Reflect","paras":["What stayed the same across the three explanations? What did you change so that you never talked down to any of the audiences?"]},{"type":"responses","id":"s4-reflect","prompts":[{"id":"reflect","label":"Your reflection"}]}]},{"id":"s5","tag":"Stage 5","title":"Produce & Reflect","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Choose one concept from Units 2 to 7 and complete an abbreviated version of the worksheet used throughout this module to design a fifteen- to twenty-minute mini-lesson for digital-native peers, in English. Your Snapshot may be in Indonesian, with an English gloss."},{"type":"table","id":"s5-lesson","header":["Worksheet part","Your draft"],"rows":[{"cells":["Concept (Units 2–7)",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Lesson identity: title; what learners already know",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["One outcome per role",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["1 · Your Digital Snapshot, and where it comes from",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["2 · Notice & Compare",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["3 · Concept Input",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["4 · Register Shift",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["5 · Produce & Reflect",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["6 · Field Journal",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["The adab connection",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"adab","label":"Before you hand in · the rubric","paras":["Tick each item when you have checked it."],"checklist":{"id":"s5-rubric","items":["The concept is explained accurately.","The example is genuinely digital-native, and says where it comes from.","At least one Unit 7 research term is used (for example elicited versus naturally occurring data, or limitation).","The adab connection is load-bearing, not decorative."]}}]},{"id":"s6","tag":"Stage 6","title":"Field Journal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"This week the entry looks inward. Log one moment from designing your mini-lesson when explaining the concept to an imagined audience exposed a gap in your own understanding. The first three columns are optional. In the *Unit focus* column, state in your own words what the gap was and how you closed it."},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"The gap that designing the lesson exposed in your own understanding, and how you closed it","labels":{"ex":"Exchange (optional)","said":"What was said (optional)","meant":"What I think was meant (optional)","focus":"Unit focus: the gap, and how I closed it"},"example":{"focus":"While preparing my mini-lesson on requests, I could not explain why “Can you close the window?” is not a question about ability. I closed the gap by writing three more examples and testing them with a classmate."}},{"type":"note","text":"**Unit focus this week:** a gap that designing the lesson exposed in your own understanding, and how you closed it."},{"type":"note","text":"**Next:** Unit 9 is the capstone: a collaborative, cross-cultural communication product that synthesizes the module’s pragmatic concepts and adab values, and brings together all six qaulan principles. Qaulan karima, introduced here, is the sixth."}]}],"references":["Bardovi-Harlig, K., & Mahan-Taylor, R. (Eds.). (2003). *Teaching pragmatics*. Office of English Language Programs, U.S. Department of State. https://americanenglish.state.gov/resources/teaching-pragmatics","Díaz-Muñoz, P., & Stecca, L. (2026). The use of ChatGPT to boost foreign language learners’ pragmatic competence: The art of complaining. In W. Fiorucci & A. Sforza Tarabochia (Eds.), *The impact of AI on language education* (pp. 179–212). Palgrave Macmillan. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-16520-6_8","Rose, K. R., & Kasper, G. (Eds.). (2001). *Pragmatics in language teaching*. Cambridge University Press.","Taguchi, N. (2015). Instructed pragmatics at a glance: Where instructional studies were, are, and should be going. *Language Teaching, 48*(1), 1–50. https://doi.org/10.1017/S0261444814000263","Thomas, J. (1983). Cross-cultural pragmatic failure. *Applied Linguistics, 4*(2), 91–112. https://doi.org/10.1093/applin/4.2.91"]},{"id":"unit09","number":9,"title":"Capstone: Respectful Cross-Cultural Digital Communication","titleId":"Proyek Akhir: Komunikasi Digital Beradab Lintas Budaya","subtitle":"Same Message, Different Culture","lead":"This is the final unit, and it introduces no new topic. You will use everything the module has given you, the pragmatic toolkit, the six qaulan, and the Panca Cinta pillars, to design a respectful cross-cultural digital communication product, and then reflect on how your own communication has changed.","lenses":{"pragmatic":"Synthesis of the concepts of Units 1 to 8","qaulan":"All six: baligha, sadida, ma’rufa, layyina, maysura, and karima","pancaCinta":"All five pillars, as an integrated whole"},"outcomes":[{"role":"Distinction","text":"Apply pragmatic concepts to explain differences between literal, inferred, implied, presupposed, and socially performed meanings in digital communication."},{"role":"Digital feature","text":"Adapt communication to digital contexts: limited non-verbal cues, asynchronous interaction, emoji, short messages, public and private audiences, and context collapse."},{"role":"Framework","text":"Integrate pragmatic knowledge with the qaulan principles and Panca Cinta values when analyzing and designing digital communication."},{"role":"Production","text":"Design, revise, and present a respectful cross-cultural digital communication product for an Indonesian–Malaysian or other intercultural audience."},{"role":"Field Journal","text":"Revisit selected Field Journal entries and reflect on how your understanding of digital meaning and intercultural communication has developed."}],"stages":[{"id":"project","tag":"The project","title":"Same Message, Different Culture","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In groups, create a short digital communication scenario involving people from different cultural backgrounds, preferably an Indonesian–Malaysian interaction. The scenario should contain a realistic possibility of pragmatic or cultural misunderstanding. Choose one of these situations:"},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["A group assignment discussion","Asking a lecturer for an extension","Inviting someone to an event","Refusing a request","Giving feedback","Apologizing for a late reply","Responding to a social-media comment","Discussing a group deadline","**Cinta Lingkungan:** responding to a post about waste, flooding, or a campus clean-up in a shared Indonesian–Malaysian group","**Cinta Tanah Air:** responding to an online argument about whose food, song, or festival it is, without stereotyping"]},{"type":"p","text":"Whatever you choose, each group names the Panca Cinta pillar its scenario engages most, so that the class as a whole covers all five. You then work through four moves:"},{"type":"table","id":"project-moves","header":["1 · Analyze","2 · Identify","3 · Revise","4 · Explain"],"rows":[{"cells":["Analyze the misunderstanding.","Identify the pragmatic issues.","Revise the communication.","Explain the changes."]}],"strip":true},{"type":"adab","label":"The goal","paras":["Students demonstrate that pragmatic knowledge can be used not only to analyze communication, but also to communicate more responsibly across cultures."]},{"type":"responses","id":"project-plan","prompts":[{"id":"scenario","label":"Our scenario"},{"id":"pillar","label":"The Panca Cinta pillar our scenario engages most"}]}]},{"id":"workflow","tag":"Workflow","title":"From challenge to solution","blocks":[{"type":"image","src":"assets/u9-workflow.png","alt":"Project Workflow (Unit 9): eight steps: spot it, read it, analyse it, check it, rewrite it, test it, reflect, showcase.","caption":"**Figure 9.1.** The project workflow, from a real cross-cultural digital communication challenge to a respectful and responsible solution."},{"type":"h2","num":"","title":"Final product options"},{"type":"p","text":"Choose one:"},{"type":"table","id":"workflow-options","header":["Option","What it shows"],"rows":[{"cells":["A · Short video","A 2–4 minute role-play showing: misunderstanding → analysis → improved communication."]},{"cells":["B · Digital infographic","Original → what went wrong? → pragmatic analysis → revised version → reflection."]},{"cells":["C · Chat simulation","A realistic chat with annotations showing the relevant pragmatic features."]},{"cells":["D · Mini online forum","A short simulated cross-cultural discussion, followed by a pragmatic analysis."]}],"keyCol":true},{"type":"responses","id":"workflow-choice","prompts":[{"id":"option","label":"Our chosen option, and why"}]}]},{"id":"tracker","tag":"Tracker","title":"Project tracker","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Use this page to keep your group’s project on track. The eight steps follow the workflow figure; the checklist follows the five requirements. It is saved on this computer, so each group member keeps their own copy."},{"type":"tracker","id":"capstone-tracker","steps":[{"id":"spot","title":"Spot it","desc":"Identify a realistic cross-cultural digital communication problem."},{"id":"read","title":"Read it","desc":"Examine what is said, what is meant, and the context."},{"id":"analyse","title":"Analyse it","desc":"Apply relevant pragmatic concepts from previous units."},{"id":"check","title":"Check it","desc":"Consider cultural differences, digital context, and adab."},{"id":"rewrite","title":"Rewrite it","desc":"Create a clearer, more respectful message."},{"id":"test","title":"Test it","desc":"Get peer feedback and revise the product."},{"id":"reflect","title":"Reflect","desc":"Connect the project to previous learning and Field Journal entries."},{"id":"showcase","title":"Showcase","desc":"Present the final digital communication product."}],"requirements":["An original digital exchange (6 to 10 messages, with English gloss)","A pragmatic analysis (analysis sheet)","A cultural analysis (what each reader could understand)","A revised message","A final reflection (each student answers the seven questions)"]}]},{"id":"requirements","tag":"Requirements","title":"What your product must contain","blocks":[{"type":"h2","num":"1","title":"An original digital exchange"},{"type":"p","text":"Create a realistic exchange of about 6 to 10 messages in the scenario’s languages (Indonesian, Malay, English, or a mix), with an English gloss for each Indonesian or Malay message. Construct or adapt it so that no real person can be identified, and label it as constructed or adapted; any real exchange follows the data-ethics policy (retype, anonymize, share only with consent). Formats: WhatsApp or Telegram chat, email, Instagram comment thread, online class discussion, or group chat."},{"type":"h2","num":"2","title":"A pragmatic analysis: apply the concepts of the earlier units (analysis sheet, next tab)"},{"type":"h2","num":"3","title":"A cultural analysis"},{"type":"p","text":"Identify what a Malaysian reader could understand, and what an Indonesian reader could understand."},{"type":"define","label":"Example · the same words, two readings","paras":["“Seminar itu percuma.” In Indonesian it can mean *that seminar is pointless*; in Malay it means *that seminar is free of charge*.","**Avoid generalizing.** Do not say “Malaysians always…” or “Indonesians always…”. Frame differences as possible cultural or contextual expectations, recognizing that individuals vary."]},{"type":"h2","num":"4","title":"A revised message"},{"type":"p","text":"Revise the message to show clarity, pragmatic appropriateness, cultural awareness, empathy, and adab. It need not become more formal: a message to a friend can remain casual, one to a lecturer formal, and a group-chat message short."},{"type":"task","label":"The key question","paras":["“Will the intended audience reasonably understand my meaning, and is the way I communicate it respectful and appropriate?”"]},{"type":"h2","num":"5","title":"A final reflection: each student answers the seven questions (reflection sheet, next tab)"}]},{"id":"analysis","tag":"Analysis sheet","title":"Pragmatic analysis and final reflection","blocks":[{"type":"table","id":"analysis-sheet","header":["Concept","Ask","Your analysis"],"rows":[{"cells":["Context","Who is communicating? What is their relationship and situation?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Deixis & Reference","What words depend on context, such as *you*, *we*, *here*, *tomorrow*, or *that*?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Entailment & Presupposition","What information is stated or assumed?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Implicature","What is suggested but not directly stated?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Speech Acts","What is each participant trying to do?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Politeness / Impoliteness","How are respect, disagreement, friendliness, or face managed?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Discourse","How does the wider conversation influence interpretation?",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"table","id":"reflection-sheet","header":["Final reflection · each student","Your answer"],"rows":[{"cells":["What caused the original misunderstanding?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Which pragmatic concepts helped us analyze it?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["How did cultural or contextual expectations affect interpretation?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["What did we change in the revised communication?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Which qaulan principle(s) guided our choices?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["Which Panca Cinta pillar did our scenario engage most, and how?",{"input":true}]},{"cells":["What have I learned about my own digital communication?",{"input":true}]}],"keyCol":true,"tall":true},{"type":"tip","label":"Did you know?","text":"A shared language is not a shared pragmatics. Indonesian and Malay overlap heavily, but a word can differ in meaning, so read your message as someone who does not share your context would.","lines":[["Seminar itu percuma.","Indonesian: the seminar is pointless"],["Seminar itu percuma.","Malay: the seminar is free of charge"]]}]},{"id":"qaulan","tag":"Qur’anic anchors","title":"The six qaulan, as a reference card","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Choose the qaulan principle(s) that fit your communication situation, and justify the choice in your reflection."},{"type":"anchor","label":"Qur’anic anchors · the six qaulan","paras":["**Qaulan baligha** · QS. An-Nisa 4:63 (Unit 2) · effective, well-aimed speech. [[Will the intended reader understand my meaning?]]","**Qaulan sadida** · QS. Al-Ahzab 33:70 (Unit 3) · truthful, correct speech. [[Is what I say, and report, accurate and honest?]]","**Qaulan ma’rufa** · QS. An-Nisa 4:5 (Unit 5) · good, fitting speech. [[Is it fitting to the person, the relationship, and the occasion?]]","**Qaulan layyina** · QS. Taha 20:44 (Unit 6) · gentle speech. [[Does it soften the threat to the other person’s face?]]","**Qaulan maysura** · QS. Al-Isra 17:28 (Unit 6) · easy, gentle speech. [[If I must refuse, do I do it in gentle, easy words?]]","**Qaulan karima** · QS. Al-Isra 17:23 (Unit 8) · good speech. [[Can I correct or disagree without shaming?]]","Terjemah Kemenag. Tafsir Kemenag: quran.kemenag.go.id"],"phrases":[{"verse":"an-nisa-4-63","from":-3,"n":3},{"verse":"al-ahzab-33-70","from":-2,"n":2},{"verse":"an-nisa-4-5","from":-2,"n":2},{"verse":"taha-20-44","from":2,"n":2},{"verse":"al-isra-17-28","from":-2,"n":2},{"verse":"al-isra-17-23","from":-2,"n":2},null]},{"type":"pull","label":"In one sentence","text":"Students integrate pragmatic analysis, digital communication skills, all six qaulan principles, and all five Panca Cinta pillars to design respectful and responsible cross-cultural digital communication."}]},{"id":"assessment","tag":"Assessment","title":"How the capstone is assessed","blocks":[{"type":"table","id":"assessment-rubric","header":["Criterion","Look for"],"rows":[{"cells":["Pragmatic understanding","Accurate and relevant use of concepts from previous units"]},{"cells":["Context & cultural awareness","Recognizes the role of relationship, situation, platform, and cultural expectations"]},{"cells":["Digital communication","Uses the selected digital platform realistically"]},{"cells":["Clarity","Revised message communicates the intended meaning more effectively"]},{"cells":["Adab & empathy","Demonstrates respect, consideration, tolerance, and responsibility; justifies the qaulan principle(s) chosen for the situation; names the Panca Cinta pillar the scenario engages most"]},{"cells":["Integration","Combines several pragmatic concepts rather than treating them as isolated labels"]},{"cells":["Creativity","Scenario and presentation are engaging and realistic"]},{"cells":["Reflection","Clearly explains learning and changes in communication practice"]}],"keyCol":true}]},{"id":"journal","tag":"Field Journal","title":"Final reflection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Return to two or three entries you collected during the module. The first three columns of the log (*Exchange*, *What was said*, *What I think was meant*) are optional this week, and the *Unit focus* column carries your answer to the prompt:"},{"type":"task","label":"Prompt","paras":["“What can I notice now that I could not notice at the beginning of the module?”"]},{"type":"p","text":"Reflect on how your ability has changed in recognizing:"},{"type":"list","style":"bullet","items":["implied meaning","assumptions","contextual clues","speech acts","politeness","cultural differences","potential digital misunderstandings"]},{"type":"journal","unitFocus":"What I notice now that I could not notice at the beginning of the module","labels":{"ex":"Exchange (optional)","said":"What was said (optional)","meant":"What I think was meant (optional)","focus":"Unit focus: what I notice now"},"example":{"focus":"At the start of the module I read a short “It’s fine.” as agreement. Now I notice the clues around it: the long message before it, the shorter reply, the missing thanks. Before I reply, I ask what was implied and how polite my answer needs to be."}},{"type":"note","text":"Your Field Journal becomes evidence of learning progression across the whole module."}]}],"references":[]}]};